\par\par \b \uc2 ASIAN MORNING BRIEFING: US Stocks Tumble To Fourth Straight Weekly Decline\b0\par\par\uc2 3,029 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 August 2011\par 00:20\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DJIA      10817.65 -172.93  -1.57% \par Nasdaq     2341.84  -38.59  -1.62% \par S&P 500    1123.53  -17.12  -1.50% \par FTSE 100   5040.76  -51.47  -1.01% \par Xetra DAX  5480.00 -122.80  -2.19% \par  \par 2Y Tsy  100 11/32 flat  0.201% flat \par 5Y Tsy  102 28/32 -1/32 0.900% +0.7BP \par 10Y Tsy 100 16/32 +4/32 2.069% -1.4BP \par  \par USD/JPY    76.75-78  -0.32% \par Range      76.75-76.60 \par EUR/USD    1.4383-86  +0.03% \par Range      1.4393-1.4372 \par AUD/USD    1.0402-06  -0.03% \par Range      1.0438-1.0406 \par  \par USD/JPY 1M Vol 11.65/12.05% \par EUR/USD 1M Vol 13.38/13.82% \par AUD/USD 1M Vol 15.70/16.70% \par  \par Comex Gold          $1,852.20 +$30.20 \par Nymex Crude (NY)     $82.26    -$0.12 \par  \par  \par U.S. SUMMARY & ASIAN OUTLOOK: \par STOCKS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. stocks closed lower Friday and notched their fourth straight losing week, as investors continued worrying about a potential global recession and the health of the European banking system.\f28 \par \par \uc2 The Dow Jones Industrial Average closed down 172.93 points, or 1.57%, at 10817.65. The index swung 284.99 points from its session highs to the lows in another volatile session. The zigzag action comes after the blue-chip index tumbled 419.63 points Thursday. The 5.19% drop over Thursday and Friday's trading sessions is the biggest two-day percentage drop since March 2, 2009.\par \par \uc2 For the week, the Dow tumbled 4% and is down 15% over the last four weeks. The index is down 6.6% for the year.\par \par \uc2 Hewlett-Packard Co\uc2 . (HPQ) was by far the Dow's biggest decliner Friday as the stock tumbled to a six-year low. Investors fretted about the world's largest personal-computer maker's extensive plans to reshape its business model. Shares dropped $5.91, or 20%, to $23.60. The stock took 45 points off the price-weighted Dow.\par \par \uc2 Fellow blue chip International Business Machines Corp. (IBM) slumped $6.29, or 3.8%, to $157.54. H-P and IBM combined to account for more than half of the Dow's declines Friday.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  500-stock index dropped 17.12 points, or 1.5%, to 1123.53, led lower by technology and financial stocks. All 10 of the S&P 500's sectors closed in negative territory. The index has tumbled 16% over its four-week losing skid.\par \par \uc2 The Nasdaq Composite slid 38.59 points, or 1.62%, to 2341.84, a fresh 2011 closing low. The technology-heavy index is down 18% over the last four weeks. For the year, it's off 12%.\par \par \uc2 Friday's lull in market-driven headlines did little to assuage investor concerns about political gridlock in Washington, Europe's sovereign-debt troubles and the worsening U.S. economy.\par \par \uc2 "The market hasn't found its footing yet," said Todd Colvin, vice president at \uc2 MF Global\uc2 . "Investor angst remains very high. Good volume associated with a down move typically isn't a fluke. We're probably in store for lower prices from here."\par \par \uc2 Optimism early in the week proved fleeting as worries about global economic growth returned. A jarring mix of global growth warnings from several investment banks and broader systemic financial problems have prompted investors to recalibrate their expectations.\par \par \uc2 The big worry now is that the problems surrounding European banks could spillover into the U.S. banking system. Investors are also concerned that European leaders aren't responding adequately to the debt crisis roiling the euro zone.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. is on "the brink of recession mode," said John Coyne, president of Brinker Capital. "The dead-cat bounce is over."\par \par \uc2 China's shares ended lower for the fourth consecutive session Friday, following an overnight slide in the U.S. markets as well as pre-weekend jitters over potential tightening measures in China.\par \par \uc2 Li Lei, an analyst at Gold State Securities, said emerging economies, such as China, still have relatively healthier fiscal fundamentals, and their stock markets are likely to start decoupling from those in the West. "(However) in recent sessions, there have also been some uncertainties about whether China will hike interest rates again," Li said.\par \par \uc2 Hong Kong shares fell Friday amid renewed fears of a global recession as investors faced a grim combination of disappointing U.S. economic data and escalating concerns about Europe's banks.\par \par \uc2 South China Research said that in the short run "weak sentiment and risk aversion should continue to dominate the market." It tipped the benchmark index could test its Aug. 9 low of 18,868, which was its lowest level since July 2009.\par \par \uc2 Tokyo stocks fell sharply Friday as further signs of weakness in U.S. manufacturing activity clouded prospects for Japan's economic recovery, spurring a sell-off in exporter shares.\par \par \uc2 "The deterioration in the global economy will shatter expectations for a Japanese V-shaped recovery," Yutaka Shiraki, a senior equity strategist at Mitsubishi UFJ Morgan Stanley Securities, said, adding that the early recovery scenario had been the main support factor for Japanese equities.\par \par \uc2 This week brings results from \uc2 Industrial & Commercial Bank of China\uc2  Ltd., \uc2 Bank of China Ltd\uc2 . and \uc2 Agricultural Bank of China Ltd\uc2 ., among others, with all of them expected to report profit growth of at least 20% from the year-earlier period on strong interest rate and fee incomes.\par \par \uc2 "While we have a positive rating for the sector, we acknowledge that market risk premiums have increased on asset-quality and economic growth concerns in China," Daiwa Capital Markets analysts Grace Wu and Queenie Poon wrote in a report.\par \par \uc2 Over the weekend, \uc2 China Construction Bank Corp\uc2 . (0939.HK) said its first-half net profit rose 31%, as Beijing's anti-inflation efforts enabled the country's second-largest lender by assets to charge higher rates from borrowers. The state-run bank said it will spend $150 million to set up a wholly owned subsidiary in Moscow and inject CNY2.89 billion ($452 million) into its newly established insurance unit, CCB Life Insurance Co.\par \par \uc2 Also over the weekend, Chinese Vice Premier Wang Qishan said China's financial sector needs to boost its support for the agriculture sector and small firms and ensure the latter's "reasonable" funding demand is satisfied.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Beijing's plan to allow exchange-traded funds comprising Hong Kong-listed stocks to be sold to mainland investors wouldn't cause huge volatility in the city's equities market, Hong Kong's Secretary for Financial Services and the Treasury, KC Chan, said in a radio program Saturday.\par \par \uc2 The implementation of the exchange traded funds will be subject to the approval of the Chinese securities regulator and the investors are rather rational, Chan said, so he doesn't believe the new measure will cause volatility in Hong Kong's stock market.\par \par \uc2 Crude futures settled nearly flat in U.S. trading Friday, pausing after steep drops earlier this week as traders weigh the threat of an economic slowdown against persistent oil demand.\par \par \uc2 Gold catapulted to another record in U.S. trading Friday as fears about the U.S. economy and concerns about Europe's banks added to bullish sentiment that gold is set to reach $2,000 a troy ounce.\par \par \uc2 Base metals ended a volatile week in positive territory on the London Metal Exchange Friday, supported by bargain hunting at lower prices and a softer dollar, although gains were pared in late trade as stock markets slipped back into the red.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Unconvinced by the near constant risk of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , traders sent the dollar to a new record low against the yen Friday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar fell as low as Y75.94 before clawing back most of those losses. The dollar also weakened against the Swiss franc, though the possible threat of action by its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  also looms over the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Analysts say Friday's dollar drop below Y76 for the first time was likely pushed by technical triggers--when automatic trades set up around the previous record low of Y76.25 were hit, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  pair took a dive.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar's consistent drop against the yen has pushed the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  multiple times in recent months and forced government officials to talk tough about fighting the move, but so far they haven't been able to cause much more than a temporary pause in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's rise.\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  earlier this month only provided temporary relief. The country's Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda said Japan might look to take more long-term steps to provide manufacturers stung by the strong \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  relief, including possible measures in the country's third supplementary budget.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Early Friday, Japan's top \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy bureaucrat said that fundamentals weren't driving demand for the yen, an oft-repeated piece of rhetoric. There's an element of speculation behind the trading, said Takehiko Nakao, vice finance minister for international affairs.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Asked about the yen's strength, Noda told NHK Television that he would "respond with sense of urgency in the new week." The Finance Ministry is responsible for deciding on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , which is then executed by the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  investors are fully ready for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ; we'll get set from Monday early morning," said Yuji Saito, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market director at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Credit Agricole\f28 \uc2 \f2  in Tokyo.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Japan's policymakers have sat on their hands since their failed \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to weaken the yen earlier this month, contemplating what to do next," said Capital Economics in a report over the weekend. "It looks increasingly likely that they will be forced to act again sooner rather than later," the firm said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Saito said Japan may make one concerted effort to bring the dollar back to Y80, although he said achieving such a large price swing would be costly, requiring massive volumes of yen selling.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Switzerland is facing an eerily similar fate as investors pour into the franc and out of the dollar and euro. The strong \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  hurt the countries' exporters who face competition in international markets from countries where goods are cheaper.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Both the U.S. and Europe are facing major economic questions and struggling under mounting debt, issues that have made the franc and yen unattractive options for investors. [ 21-08-11 2220GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Rumors have swirled for more than a week that the SNB might peg its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  or at least narrow its trading range to a specific band against the euro to weaken the franc. Those threats weakened the franc for a short while, but it's already clear that investors are ramping up their one-way bets favoring the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  again.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The "SNB is reluctant to do the next step to take a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  peg or band," said Marc Chandler, global head of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Brown Brothers Harriman\f28 \uc2 \f2  in New York.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Late Friday, the euro was at $1.4396 from $1.4334 late Thursday, according to EBS via CQG. The dollar was at Y76.54 from Y76.57, while the euro was at Y110.20 from Y109.75. The U.K. pound was at $1.6479 from $1.6513. The dollar was at CHF0.7848 from CHF0.7941.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The ICE Dollar Index, which tracks the U.S. dollar against a basket of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , was at 73.990 from 74.244.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par BONDS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The 30-year bond's price rose for a fourth straight session and was Friday's best performer. In contrast, many shorter-dated notes traded flat to slightly weaker, a day after the rally that sent benchmark yields to historic lows.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Until recently, the long bond has lagged shorter-dated peers partly due to the political impasse to shrink the nation's deep fiscal shortfall. Many investors were also concerned that the Federal Reserve's ultra-loose monetary policy would spark inflation, a main threat to the long bond's value over time.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Yet over the past two weeks, the long bond has been catching up and buying picked up speed during the week as growth fears are overriding inflation concerns. With yields on shorter-dated notes trading near historic lows, many investors are forced to pile into the 30-year bond to grab some extra returns, even as that means buying bonds at lofty prices.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The problems that drove us to lower yields are still here. The economy is slowing not just in the U.S. but also globally, and the euro-zone debt problems are not getting better," said Mary Ann Hurley, vice president of trading in Seattle at \f28 \uc2 \f2 D.A. Davidson & Co\f28 \uc2 \f2 . "With short end yields having little room to fall, long bonds should be the beneficiary here."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In late Friday afternoon trading, the 30-year bond was 31/32 higher to yield 3.393%. Bond yields move inversely to their prices. The yield fell 31 basis points during the week, more than any other maturity in the Treasury market.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The benchmark 10-year note was 4/32 higher to yield 2.069%. The yield dropped from 2.239% a week earlier and fell to as low as 1.974% Thursday. The two-year note was flat to yield 0.201%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Martin Mitchell, head government bond trader at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Stifel Nicolaus & Co\f28 \uc2 \f2 . in Baltimore, said that the 30-year bond's yield is still far above the record low of 2.505% hit in December 2008 after the demise of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Lehman Brothers\f28 \uc2 \f2 . That means the 30-year yield "has room to fall" and the decline will pull the rest of the Treasury curve with it.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Many investors said an extra boost for long-dated Treasurys could come from the Fed in coming weeks. Speculation has increased that Fed Chairman Ben Bernanke may signal further monetary stimulus when he speaks at the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's annual conference to be held late this week in Jackson Hole, Wyo.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 An option that has gained traction is for the Fed to buy more long-dated Treasurys using proceeds from its maturing Treasury and mortgage bond holdings. That would help push down yields on long-dated Treasurys, which in turn would lower borrowing costs for consumers and corporate finances.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The 10-year Japanese government bond yield fell to a nine-month low Friday, as stock market plunges around the world prompted demand for safe-haven assets.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A focus for the JGB market in the coming week is who will officially run for the ruling Democratic Party of Japan's leadership election, which is expected to be held around the end of the month.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 If current Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda, who's in favor of quake-reconstruction tax hikes, stays in the lead, investors will likely feel comfortable buying JGBs, said Deutsche Securities strategist Makoto Yamashita.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Any tax hikes will likely help reduce reliance on JGB issuance which is favorable for the market's supply and demand balance.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China government bonds ended slightly higher Friday as tumbles in Wall Street and European equity markets Thursday boosted domestic investors' interest in safe-haven fixed-income products, a Beijing-based trader with a local insurer said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investors are also focusing on whether the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will continue to lift the yields on its bills issued in the open market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the coming week, he added.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Expectations of an imminent interest rate hike are likely to heighten if the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  bill rates keep rising."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Zhang Xiaoqiang, vice head of the National Development and Reform Commission said at a forum over the weekend China still faces high inflationary pressure, but based on current conditions it can achieve stable economic growth. He didn't elaborate.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Among data due Monday, Thailand's economic growth likely decelerated in the second quarter as the adverse effect from supply disruptions in the auto industry took its toll. According to the median forecast of seven economists polled by Dow Jones, second-quarter GDP likely grew 0.4% on the quarter, down from a 2.0% rise in the first quarter. On the year, GDP likely rose 3.55%, up from the first quarter's 3.0% rise.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Hong Kong's headline inflation likely surged to 8.2% in July, its highest in more than 15 years and up from June's 5.6% reading, according to the median forecast of eight economists polled by Dow Jones.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par TOP HEADLINES: \par EU, German Leaders Harden Opposition To Euro-Zone Bonds \par Merkel Expects Lawmakers To OK EFSF Changes \par Greece Calls On Europe To Give Clear Message On Finland Deal \par Finance Ministry Expects Euro-Zone Bonds To Cost Germany Billions - Spiegel \par Record High Level For Yen Raises \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Stakes, Pressures BOJ \par Greek Prime Minister Rules Out Early Elections - Report \par Biden: U.S. 'Never Will Default' But Has To Tackle Deficit \par BOJ's Yamaguchi: China Needs To Reform Exchange Rate Mechanism In Long Run \par China Vice Premier: To Boost Financial Support For Agriculture Sector, Small Firms \par China NDRC Official: Inflationary Pressures Are Still High \par China President: China, US Should Jointly Boost Global Economic Recovery - Xinhua \par Japan Ready To Take Solo Action To Stem Yen's Rise - Nikkei \par EX-PBOC Advisor: China Has Room To Use Quantitative Monetary Tools \par China Less Likely To Raise Banks' Reserve Ratio In 2H 2011 -Paper \par Ex-PBOC Vice Gov Wu: Chinese Banks May Need CNY400B-500B In Next 5 Years \par US Says Libyan Rebel Offensive To Take Tripoli Is Under Way \par BofA Job Cuts, Asset Sales Alleviate Skepticism \par H-P Shares Sink To 6-Year Low \par SNB Reports Dollar Borrowing, Banks Deny \par Greek Economy May Contract By 5.2% In 2011 \par S&P Cuts Venezuela A Notch \par Pianalto Says US Recession Worse Than Thought \par Frontpoint Plans To Shed Strategic Credit Fund \par Investors Turn Positive On Euro \par  \par  \par SCHEDULE OF MAIN EVENTS (times in GMT): \par 0230  Thailand     2Q  GDP \par 0500  Japan        Jul Supermarket sales \par 0800  Taiwan       Jul Employment / Unemployment \par 0830  Hong Kong    Jul CPI \par 0900  Malaysia     Aug International Reserves, middle of month \par 1230  US           July Chicago Fed National Activity Index \par  \par -By Helen Ozolins, Dow Jones Newswires; +64 4 471 5990; helen.ozolins@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 21-08-11 2220GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1160 4119   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110821e78l0001t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
