\par\par \b \uc2 INTERVIEW: Japan's Top  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2 -Policy Bureaucrat Says Yen Is Not For "Flights To Safety"\b0\par\par\uc2 903 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 August 2011\par 14:33\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 -- Japan top \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -policy bureaucrat Nakao says no reason for yen to be used in a "flight to safety"\par \par \uc2 -- Says will take "appropriate action" on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  if needed\par \par \uc2 -- Says recent yen rises do not reflect economic fundamentals and have an "element of speculation"\par \par \uc2 -- Says \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  not aimed at targeting specific yen levels\par \par \uc2 -- Says \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  not to be used "as a daily tool"\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Takashi Nakamichi and Tatsuo Ito \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Japan's top \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 -policy bureaucrat complained Friday about global investors treating the yen as a "flight-to-safety \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 " during times of global economic distress.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We don't think recent yen moves really reflect economic fundamentals,"Takehiko Nakao, vice finance minister for international affairs, told Dow Jones Newswires in an interview Friday. There is an "element of speculation" behind it, he added.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Even so, the veteran finance official who took over the powerful job steering Japan's global financial policies around two weeks ago said the government has no plans to start a sustained \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  campaign to steer the yen to specific levels but will nevertheless take "appropriate action" when it is deemed necessary.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Nakao said that "we don't have plans to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  often." He added: "We don't use \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as a daily tool."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 He said that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's economy "is still suffering" from the March 11 earthquake and tsunami. Combined with that, the nation's structural issues, such as its shrinking population and looming debt problem mean that "there is no reason that the yen should be regarded as a flight-to-safety \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ," he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 His comments shed new light on how dissatisfied Tokyo is with overseas investors using the Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as a parking space when global economic turmoil arises.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The soaring yen, and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's constant threat of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , comes amid the backdrop of a broader financial volatility across the globe, and across markets ranging from \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to stocks to bonds.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 While some analysts have said that the current period could mirror the Lehman crisis of 2008, Nakao said it isn't that severe now. "In the Lehman case, we didn't know where the problems were, where the bad assets were. But this time it is much more transparent."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Nakao also defended the U.S. government's economic policies in the wake of the recent downgrade of Treasurys. He said the latest debt-ceiling deal is "a really substantive package" that includes "very concrete actions" to reduce the deficit.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 And he said he didn't see the pre-eminent status of the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  or U.S. debt in global markets changing any time soon. "I myself believe that the U.S. dollar will remain the most important single key \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the world for the foreseeable future," he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Nakao, 55, joined the ministry in 1978 after earning an economics degree from the University of Tokyo. Having spent three years as an economist at the International Monetary Fund and two years at the Japanese embassy in Washington, Nakao is considered to be one of the most knowledgeable U.S. specialists within the ministry.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Just a few days after he took his post, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's government \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market on Aug. 4, dumping an estimated Y4.6 trillion, the largest on record for a single day.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But the yen climbed back to its pre-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  levels within days of the action. While analysts said the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was ineffective, Nakao seems to think the move had some success. "At least...we sent a very clear signal of the authorities' intention to discourage speculation."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As the right hand man of the finance minister on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy, Nakao plays a key role in deciding and implementing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Nakao declined to say whether the U.S. or Europe supported Japan's latest move. But he said he doesn't think unilateral \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  "is precluded as a tool to stabilize the markets," though he emphasized: "Consultation with other nations is required even for solo \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Nakao said that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's economy has also shown some strengths, such as faster-than-expected recovery in the quake-damaged supply chains.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As Japan's top financial diplomat, Nakao also said he wants to build stronger ties with increasingly important Asian neighbors.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan and Asian economies are now "on an equal footing, not in a simple relationship of capital providers and recipients, so I think it is very constructive to have closer communications and consultations with Asian countries," he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Defying the stereotypical image of a Japanese bureaucrat, Nakao likes to talk about the time he spends at home as well as at the office. "I want to have a better work-life balance in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ," said Nakao, a father of two boys.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We shouldn't take it for granted that people will stay until very late at night." He said he tries to leave the building at 7 p.m. on business days and prepares meals for his family over the weekend. "One thing I feel is difficult about this job is that I must go abroad so often so I cannot cook lunches and dinners, which I do every weekend." he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Takashi Nakamichi and Tatsuo Ito, Dow Jones Newswires; +81-3-6269-2781; takashi.nakamichi@dowjones.com [ 19-08-11 1233GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110819e78j000wz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
