\par\par \b \uc2 ASIAN MORNING BRIEFING:US Stocks Down on European Bank Worries\b0\par\par\uc2 1,798 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 August 2011\par 23:41\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DJIA               10817.65   -172.93    -1.57% \par Nasdaq              2341.84    -38.59    -1.62% \par S&P 500             1123.53    -17.12    -1.50% \par FTSE 100            5040.76    -51.47    -1.01% \par Xetra DAX           5480.00   -122.80    -2.19% \par  \par 2Y Tsy   100 11/32   flat   0.201    0 \par 5Y Tsy   102 28/32   -1/32  0.900%  +0.7 \par 10 Tsy   100 16/32   +4/32  2.069%  -1.4 \par  \par USD/JPY Japan         76.57-59  -0.08% \par Range                 75.96-76.94 \par EUR/USD Euro          1.4332-35  +0.47% \par Range                 1.4260-1.4452 \par AUD/USD Australia     1.0389-94  +0.20% \par Range                 1.0318-1.0479 \par  \par Comex Gold          $1852.20    +$30.20 \par Nymex Crude (NY)      $82.26    -$0.12 \par  \par U.S. SUMMARY: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 STOCKS:\f28 \par \par \uc2 U.S. stocks closed down Friday and notched its fourth straight losing week, as investors continued worrying about a potential global recession and the health of the European banking system.\par \par \uc2 The Dow Jones Industrial Average closed down 172.93 points, or 1.57%, to 10817.65. The index swung 284.99 points from its session highs to the lows in another volatile session. The zigzag action comes after the blue-chip index tumbled 419.63 points on Thursday. The 5.19% drop over Thursday and Friday's trading is the biggest two-day percentage drop since March 2, 2009.\par \par \uc2 For the week, the Dow tumbled 4% and it's down 15% over the last four weeks. The index is down 6.6% for the year.\par \par \uc2 Hewlett-Packard Co\uc2 . (HPQ) was by far the Dow's biggest decliner on Friday as the stock tumbled to a six-year low. Investors fretted about the world's largest personal-computer maker's extensive plans to reshape its business model. Shares dropped $5.91, or 20%, to $23.60. The stock took 45 points off the price-weighted Dow.\par \par \uc2 Fellow blue chip International Business Machines Corp. (IBM) also slumped $6.29, or 3.8%, to $157.54. H-P and IBM combined to account for more than half of the Dow's declines on Friday.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  500-stock index dropped 17.12 points, or 1.5%, to 1123.53, led lower by technology and financial stocks. All 10 of the S&P 500's sectors closed in negative territory. The index has tumbled 16% over its four-week losing skid.\par \par \uc2 The Nasdaq Composite slid 38.59 points, or 1.62%, to 2341.84, a fresh 2011 closing low. The technology-heavy index is down 18% over the last four weeks. For the year, it's off 12%.\par \par \uc2 Friday's lull in market-driven headlines did little to assuage investor concerns about political gridlock in Washington, Europe's sovereign-debt troubles and the worsening U.S. economy.\par \par \uc2 "The market hasn't found its footing yet," said Todd Colvin, vice president at \uc2 MF Global\uc2 . "Investor angst remains very high. Good volume associated with a down move typically isn't a fluke. We're probably in store for lower prices from here."\par \par \uc2 Stocks started the week on a strong note, inspired in part by hopes that Europe's leaders may be nearing an accord to help resolve the spreading debt crisis. A flurry of takeover activity in the U.S. also underscored the resilience of U.S. companies despite the slowing economy.\par \par \uc2 The optimism, however, proved fleeting as worries about global economic growth have returned. A jarring mix of global growth warnings from several investment banks and broader systemic financial problems have prompted investors to recalibrate their expectations.\par \par \uc2 The big worry now is that the problems surrounding European banks could spillover into the U.S. banking system. Investors are also concerned that European leaders aren't responding adequately to the debt crisis roiling the euro zone.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 :\par \par \uc2 Unconvinced by the near constant risk of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , traders sent the dollar to a new record low against the yen Friday.\par \par \uc2 The dollar fell as low as Y75.94 before clawing back most of those losses. The dollar also weakened against the Swiss franc, though its \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is also looming over the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market with the possible threat of action.\par \par \uc2 There are "risks at these levels that the [\uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  or Bank of Japan] will step in over the weekend," said Omer Esiner, chief market analyst at \uc2 Commonwealth \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Washington. "It highlights the extent of how in demand the market is for safe havens."\par \par \uc2 Analysts say Friday's dollar drop below Y76 for the first time was likely pushed by technical triggers--when automatic trades set up around the previous record low of Y76.25 were hit, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pair took a dive.\par \par \uc2 The dollar's consistent drop against the yen has pushed the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  multiple times in recent months and forced government officials to talk tough about fighting the move, but so far they haven't been able to cause much more than a temporary pause in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  earlier this month only provided temporary relief. The country's Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda said Japan might look to take more long-term steps to provide manufacturers stung by the strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  relief, including possible measures in the country's third supplementary budget.\par \par \uc2 Earlier Friday, Japan's top \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy bureaucrat said that fundamentals were not driving demand for the yen, an oft-repeated piece of rhetoric. There's an element of speculation behind the trading, said Takehiko Nakao, vice finance minister for international affairs.\par \par \uc2 Switzerland is facing an eerily similar fate as investors pour into the franc and out of the dollar and euro. The strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  hurt the countries' exporters who face competition in international markets from countries where goods are cheaper.\par \par \uc2 Both the U.S. and Europe are facing major economic questions and struggling under mounting debt, issues that have made the franc and yen unattractive options for investors.\par \par \uc2 Rumors have swirled for more than a week that the SNB might peg its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  or at least narrow its trading range to a specific band against the euro to weaken the franc. Those threats weakened the franc for a short while, but it's already clear that investors are ramping up their one-way bets favoring the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  again.\par \par \uc2 The "SNB is reluctant to do the next step to take a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg or band," said Marc Chandler, global head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at \uc2 Brown Brothers Harriman\uc2  in New York.\par \par \uc2 Last year, the SNB tried to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  and lost a lot of money--analysts say that's kept the SNB from making any major new announcements.\par \par \uc2 Late Friday, the euro was at $1.4396 from $1.4334 late Thursday, according to EBS via CQG. The dollar was at Y76.54 from Y76.57, while the euro was at Y110.20 from Y109.75. The U.K. pound was at $1.6479 from $1.6513. The dollar was at CHF0.7848 from CHF0.7941.\par \par \uc2 BONDS:\par \par \uc2 The 30-year bond is now the sweetest spot in the safe-haven Treasury market.\par \par \uc2 Friday, the long bond's price rose for a fourth straight session and was the best performer. In contrast, many shorter-dated notes traded flat to slightly weaker, a day after the rally that sent benchmark yields to historic lows.\par \par \uc2 Until recently, the long bond has lagged shorter-dated peers partly due to the political impasse to shrink the nation's deep fiscal shortfall. Many investors were also concerned that the Federal Reserve's ultra-loose monetary policy would spark inflation, a main threat to the long bond's value over time.\par \par \uc2 Yet over the past two weeks, the long bond has been catching up and buying picked up speed this week as growth fears are overriding inflation concerns. With yields on shorter-dated notes trading near historic low levels, many investors are forced to pile into the 30-year bond to grab some extra returns, even as that means buying bonds at lofty price levels.\par \par \uc2 "The problems that drove us to lower yields are still here. The economy is slowing not just in the U.S. but also globally, and the euro-zone debt problems are not getting better," said Mary Ann Hurley, vice president of trading in Seattle at \uc2 D.A. Davidson & Co\uc2 . "With short end yields having little room to fall, long bonds should be the beneficiary here."\par \par \uc2 In late-afternoon trading, the 30-year bond was 31/32 higher to yield 3.393%. Bond yields move inversely to their prices. The yield fell 31 basis points this week, bigger than any other maturity in the Treasury market.\par \par \uc2 The benchmark 10-year note was 4/32 higher to yield 2.069%. The yield dropped from 2.239% a week ago and fell to as low as 1.974% on Thursday. The two-year note was flat to yield 0.201%.\par \par \uc2 Martin Mitchell, head government bond trader at \uc2 Stifel Nicolaus & Co\uc2 . in Baltimore, said that the 30-year bond's yield is still far above the record low of 2.505% hit in December 2008 after the demise of \uc2 Lehman Brothers\uc2 . That means the 30-year yield "has room to fall" and the decline will pull the rest of the Treasury curve with it.\par \par \uc2 Many investors said an extra boost for long-dated Treasurys could come from the Fed in coming weeks. Speculation has increased that Fed Chairman Ben Bernanke may signal further monetary stimulus when he speaks at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's annual conference to be held late next week in Jackson Hole, Wyo.\par \par \uc2 An option that has gained traction is for the Fed to buy more long-dated Treasurys using proceeds from its maturing Treasury and mortgage bond holdings. That would help push down yields on long-dated Treasurys, which in turn would lower borrowing costs for consumers and corporate finances.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par TOP HEADLINES: \par US Stocks Tumble To Fourth Straight Weekly Decline \par Bofa Job Cuts, Asset Sales Alleviate Skepticism \par H-P Shares Sink To 6-year Low \par SNB Reports Dollar Borrowing, Banks Deny \par Greek Economy May Contract By 5.2% In 2011 \par S&P Cuts Venezuela A Notch \par Pianalto Says US Recession Worse Than Thought \par Investors Turn Positive On Euro \par After Swings, Crude Ends Nearly Flat \par Icahn To Nominate Self, 10 Others, To Clorox Board \par  \par SCHEDULE OF MAIN EVENTS (times in GMT): \par 0230   Thailand  2Q GDP \par 0500   Japan     July Supermarket sales \par 0800   Taiwan    July Employment / Unemployment \par 0830   HK        July CPI \par 0900   Malaysia  August International Reserves, middle of month \par  \par  \par SCHEDULE OF MAIN EVENTS (times in GMT): \par 1230  US           July Chicago Fed National Activity Index \par                       National Activity Index \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 19-08-11 2141GMT ]\f28 \pa