\par\par \b \uc2 DJ  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2 's Noda Hints At Another  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2 , More Easing-Kyodo\b0\par\par\uc2 286 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 August 2011\par 15:29\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 DOW JONES NEWSWIRES\par \par \uc2 Japanese Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda suggested Thursday that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market again to stem the yen's rise and that the Bank of Japan may implement additional monetary easing in response to the moves of U.S. financial authorities, Kyodo News reported.\par \par \uc2 Referring to the yen's recent appreciation in a speech in the city of Chiba, Noda said, "We intend to keep monitoring moves in the market and take bold steps if necessary."\par \par \uc2 He also said the focus is on how U.S. Federal Reserve Board Chairman Ben Bernanke will explain the monetary policy of the United States later this month.\par \par \uc2 "If there are some financial moves (in the United States), I think it's probable that the Bank of Japan will take an additional monetary easing step," he said in an unusual reference as a finance minister to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's monetary policy.\par \par \uc2 "We (the Finance Ministry) will move closely together with the Bank of Japan and each will take appropriate measures at appropriate timing," he said.\par \par \uc2 On Aug. 4, Japanese monetary authorities stepped into the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in a bid to prevent the yen's further appreciation, and the BOJ decided to expand its Y40 trillion asset purchase program to Y50 trillion in an effort to support the economy following the March 11 massive earthquake and tsunami.\par \par \uc2 The dollar traded in the upper Y76 range in Tokyo trading hours Thursday, within sight of its postwar record low of Y76.25 set March 17.\par \par \uc2 -Dow Jones Newswires; 212-416-2900\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 August 18, 2011 09:29 ET (13:29 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020110818e78i004lc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
