\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ(8/19) Meeting Suggests Further Yen Moves\b0\par\par\uc2 265 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 August 2011\par 01:08\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par C3\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    (From THE WALL STREET JOURNAL) \par    By Takashi Nakamichi \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TOKYO -- Japan's top \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 -policy bureaucrat said he held a special meeting with his \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central-bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  counterpart Thursday to discuss "what could be considered" to deal with the yen's persistent strength, the latest indication that Tokyo may be contemplating renewed action to stop the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's rise.\f28 \par \par \uc2 Vice Finance Minister for International Affairs Takehiko Nakao said the yen's recent rise was the main topic in a meeting with Hiroshi Nakaso, the Bank of Japan official for international affairs, that lasted for more than 30 minutes. The basic goals of the conversation were to "analyze the current situation" and discuss "what could be considered going forward," Mr. Nakao told reporters after the session.\par \par \uc2 The unusual step of disclosing that the meeting had taken place was seen by some as a way to keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market from pushing the yen higher. Last week, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also directly contacted \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers asking for the latest rates, in the hope of discouraging yen buying by stoking fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Although analysts question whether \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  can halt the yen's rise without the help of other central banks or until fears of a global economic slowdown ease, the meeting added to speculation that the government and the BOJ might nonetheless again try some sort of joint action.\par \par \uc2 The dollar was at 76.58 yen late Thursday in New York, from 76.59 yen late Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 August 18, 2011 19:08 ET (23:08 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020110818e78i007pn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
