\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Japan MOF, BOJ officials met to discuss  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  -BOJ\b0\par\par\uc2 343 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 August 2011\par 05:28\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Did not discuss monetary policy at meeting -BOJ\par \par \uc2 * MOF official says on alert for \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves -Kyodo\par \par \uc2 (Adds quote, detail)\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Aug 18 (Reuters) - Senior finance ministry and Bank of Japan officials on Thursday met to exchange views on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said, a sign that Tokyo remains geared up for further action to stem persistent rises in the yen.\par \par \uc2 Takehiko Nakao, Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  tsar and the No. 2 bureaucrat at the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , met with his BOJ counterpart, Hiroshi Nakaso, at the BOJ's headquarters on Thursday, a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  spokesman told Reuters.\par \par \uc2 "At the meeting, the two exchanged views on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market moves," the spokesman said. They did not discuss monetary policy at the meeting, he added.\par \par \uc2 Finance ministry officials were not immediately available for comment, though Nakao was quoted by Jiji news agency as saying that the MOF and the BOJ agreed to work as one to deal with yen rises.\par \par \uc2 Nakao also told reporters on his way back from the BOJ that the government remains on alert over \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves, and that recent yen rises were volatile and speculative, according to Kyodo news agency.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  by selling its own \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and eased monetary policy on Aug. 4, but the moves have offered little respite to exporters as the yen remains near an all-time high of 76.25 to the dollar.\par \par \uc2 Japanese policymakers have repeatedly warned that they are ready to act again if yen rises are too sharp, though Tokyo has held off on direct action since then.\par \par \uc2 A similar meeting between senior MOF and BOJ officials was held in August last year, also in the wake of sharp yen rises, which led to a joint statement between the two organisations warning markets against excessive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility.\par \par \uc2 There is no plan to issue such a statement this time, the BOJ spokesman said.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Rie Ishiguro and Leika Kihara; Editing by Joseph Radford)\par \par \uc2 JAPAN-ECONOMY/MOF-BOJ  (UPDATE 1)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110821e78i0001g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
