\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 3-Japan corp mood up but exports slow as yen bites\b0\par\par\uc2 893 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 August 2011\par 02:48\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Manuf sentiment DI +6, non-manuf +7 -Reuters Tankan\par \par \uc2 * Exports -3.3 pct yr/yr versus forecast -2.4 pct yr/yr\par \par \uc2 * Strong yen, slowdown in overseas economies clouds outlook\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  still on cards as yen gains persist\par \par \uc2 * About 30 pct see power shortages pushing production abroad -poll\par \par \uc2 (Adds separate poll)\par \par \uc2 By Rie Ishiguro and Tetsushi Kajimoto\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Aug 18 (Reuters) - Japan's business mood improved in August and is expected to brighten further as manufacturers restore output to pre-disaster levels, a Reuters poll showed, but a rising yen and slackening foreign demand have started taking their toll on exports.\par \par \uc2 Companies in the Reuters Tankan survey expressed growing concern about the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise while trade data also published on Thursday showed recovery in exports slowed markedly last month when the yen soared against a weakening dollar.\par \par \uc2 Slowing exports pose a risk to the world's third-largest economy, which is about to pull out of a recession triggered by a massive earthquake and tsunami in March that caused the world's worst nuclear power crisis in 25 years, since Chernobyl.\par \par \uc2 Exports inched up from June but were down more than expected in comparison with July 2010. Shipments to China and the United States -- Japan's key markets -- both fell.\par \par \uc2 Domestic demand, driven by companies retooling and boosting output, is bolstering business sentiment and the economy is expected to bounce strongly in the present quarter.\par \par \uc2 However, the strong yen, uncertainty about global growth and the timing of government-funded rebuilding efforts are clouding the outlook.\par \par \uc2 "Reconstruction demand has kicked in at sectors such as construction, utilities and fisheries thanks largely to private-sector investments," said Minoru Masujima, director for the Cabinet Office's department of business statistics.\par \par \uc2 "Everyone has been expecting 'reconstruction demand' to boost the economy from July-September, but such a scenario is far from certain as it is unclear when the government can implement a third extra budget to fund fully-fledged rebuilding."\par \par \uc2 The government plans to spend 13 trillion yen ($169.8 billion)to rebuild coastal areas ravaged by the tsunami in addition to 6 trillion yen already earmarked for post-disaster relief and clean-up.\par \par \uc2 But it is not clear when the next emergency budget will be passed and the money will start flowing, given that the unpopular Prime Minister Naoto Kan is on his way out and it may take weeks before a new cabinet is formed.\par \par \uc2 Some companies have already benefited from private sector-driven demand, the Reuters poll showed, but a delay in government's spending could threaten a V-shaped recovery expected in the next two quarters.\par \par \uc2 Construction machinery maker \uc2 Komatsu Ltd\uc2  has reported a 24 percent rise in operating profits in April-June from a year earlier due in part to demand related to rebuilding factories and facilities damaged by the March disaster.\par \par \uc2 MANUFACTURING MOOD LAGS SERVICE SECTORS\par \par \uc2 Exports fell 3.3 percent in July from a year earlier against an 2.4 percent annual decline expected by economists, as shipments to the U.S. and China fell 8.2 percent and 1.0 percent respectively.\par \par \uc2 Compared to the previous month, exports rose 0.8 percent, slower than a 5.4 percent rise in June, \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  data showed.\par \par \uc2 The Reuters Tankan survey showed the manufacturing index, derived by subtracting the percentage of pessimistic responses from optimistic ones, rose five points from July to plus 6, still well below plus 15 seen just before the March disaster.\par \par \uc2 The index for non-manufacturers rose four points from July to plus 7, its highest level since December 2007 and exceeding the manufacturing index for the second straight month.\par \par \uc2 "That clearly reflects the impact of heightened global uncertainty due to the downgrading of the U.S. (credit rating) and the persistent sovereign debt woes in Europe," said George Worthington, chief economist at IFR Markets.\par \par \uc2 "For the moment, domestic-oriented companies appear to be more positive than export-oriented, though domestic demand conditions remain generally sluggish."\par \par \uc2 The yen , which gained about 5 percent against the dollar since early July, did not budge after the data, hovering just below the record high of 76.25 to the dollar and prompting a customary warning from Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda that he is keeping a close watch on market movements.\par \par \uc2 Tokyo \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Aug. 4 by selling a record of about 4.5 trillion yen in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, but the yen quickly climbed back against the weakening dollar to pre-\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels.\par \par \uc2 A separate Reuters survey showed 29 percent of respondents among manufacturers saw the suspension of atomic power plants as a potential factor to shift production overseas, up from 25 percent in July, highlighting worries about the hollowing out of industries amid power shortages and yen's gains.\par \par \uc2 The March 11 earthquake and tsunami triggered meltdowns \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par and radiation leaks at  a nuclear power plant in\par Fukushima  ,    shattering public\par confidence in nuclear power.  \par  Th  is  has darkened the outlook for\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 the restart of off-line reactors and raised the possibility that all of Japan's 54 reactors could be shut down by May 2012. ($1 = 76.565 Japanese Yen)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Additional reporting by Yoko Kubota and Stanley White; Editing by Tomasz Janowski and Richard Borsuk)\f28 \par \par \uc2 JAPAN-ECONOMY/ (UPDATE 3)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110818e78i00013\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
