\par\par \b \uc2 INSTANT VIEW 2-Japan corporate mood improves, July exports fall\b0\par\par\uc2 906 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 August 2011\par 02:22\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Aug 18 (Reuters) - Sentiment among Japanese manufacturers improved in August for the fourth straight month and is expected improving further in the coming quarter, a Reuters poll showed, but the outlook is murky as a soaring yen and the shaky global economy may weigh on business sentiment.\par \par \uc2 Trade data showed that Japan's exports fell in July from a year earlier at a faster pace than the previous month, as an improvement in factory output and supply constraints was offset by the global economic slowdown.\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************\par \par \uc2 KEY POINTS:\par \par \uc2 -- The Reuters Tankan manufacturing sentiment index, derived by subtracting the percentage of pessimistic responses from optimistic ones, rose 5 points from July to plus 6, meaning optimists outnumbered pessimists.\par \par \uc2 -- The index for non-manufacturers rose 4 points to plus 7, its highest since December 2007.\par \par \uc2 -- The manufacturing and service-sector sentiment indexes are expected to improve to plus 13 and 14 respectively over the next three months.\par \par \uc2 -- Exports fell 3.3 percent in July from a year earlier, compared with a median forecast for a 2.4 percent annual decline and following a 1.6 percent dip in June, \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  data showed.\par \par \uc2 -- Imports rose 9.9 percent in the year to July, less than the median estimate for a 10.9 percent annual rise.\par \par \uc2 -- The nation's trade balance registered a surplus of 72.5 billion yen ($946.9 million), compared with a median forecast for a 71.0 billion yen surplus.\par \par \uc2 COMMENTARY:\par \par \uc2 On the Reuters Tankan:\par \par \uc2 HARUMI TAGUCHI, HEAD OF INVESTMENT STRATEGY AND INFORMATION DEPT, COSMO SECURITIES, TOKYO\par \par \uc2 "What's noteworthy is the improvement in sentiment for transport machinery and precision machinery makers, as output recovers from (the quake's damage) and companies continue to boost production during the summer.\par \par \uc2 "As for the outlook, we need to be mindful of the possible effect of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves and overseas developments. If the yen stays at current high levels, it will likely hurt business sentiment."\par \par \uc2 GEORGE WORTHINGTON, IFR MARKETS, SYDNEY\par \par \uc2 "The Reuters Tankan survey for August showed a further improvement in sentiment with the highest reading for manufacturers since the devastating earthquake and resulting disasters in March.\par \par \uc2 "The diffusion index for manufacturers rose to 6 from 1 in July, but was still below the 15 posted in March, while that for non-manufacturers actually rose to a higher 7 from 3 in July--this is the first month it has been higher than the manufacturers index since October 2009 when both were still in deeply negative territory, and was the strongest positive reading since December 2007.\par \par \uc2 "That clearly reflects the impact of heightened global uncertainty due to the downgrading of the U.S. and the persistent sovereign debt woes in Europe that are damaging exporter confidence.\par \par \uc2 "And for the moment, domestic-oriented companies appear to be more positive than export-oriented, though domestic demand conditions remain generally sluggish."\par \par \uc2 On the July trade data:\par \par \uc2 TAKESHI MINAMI, CHIEF ECONOMIST, NORINCHUKIN RESEARCH INSTITUTE, TOKYO\par \par \uc2 "Export growth is recovering as supply chains are restored but the effect of the global economic slowdown may be already showing.\par \par \uc2 "The global economic outlook is a concern. I don't expect exports to fall sharply ahead but any recovery in exports will probably be very modest.\par \par \uc2 "The Japanese economy is moving in line with the Bank of Japan's forecast. But growth is probably at the lower end of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's projection. The focus will still be on risks to the outlook for exports."\par \par \uc2 MAIKO NOGUCHI, SENIOR ECONOMIST, DAIWA SECURITIES CAPITAL MARKETS, TOKYO\par \par \uc2 "Exports were a little weaker than expected. The focus is on whether foreign demand will continue to weaken amid recent signs of global economic weakness.\par \par \uc2 "The yen has appreciated over an extended period and this of course is negative for exports. But a bigger theme is the state of the global economy going forward and a potential weakening in demand for Japanese exports."\par \par \uc2 GEORGE WORTHINGTON, IFR MARKETS, SYDNEY:\par \par \uc2 "Exports remain far below pre-quake levels due to continued weakness in car and electrical machinery shipments in particular even as supply chain constraints have eased. This partly reflects the firmer yen, and suggests that producers have lost market share since the earthquake and points to a sluggish recovery for exporters, especially given the renewed weakness in demand conditions in major developed markets."\par \par \uc2 MARKET REACTION:\par \par \uc2 -- For yen updates click ; for prices click\par \par \uc2 -- For JGB updates, click , for prices click <2JGBv1>\par \par \uc2 -- For stocks click , for Nikkei average click\par \par \uc2 LINKS:\par \par \uc2 BACKGROUND:\par \par \uc2 -- The Reuters Tankan covers 200 large manufacturers and 200 non-manufacturers excluding the financial sector. A total of 258 firms responded to the poll, taken from July 28 to August 11. The monthly poll has a 95 percent correlation with the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's closely watched quarterly tankan survey.\par \par \uc2 -- \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and eased monetary policy on Aug. 4, aiming to curb a yen rise that threatened to derail its recovery from the devastating earthquake and deadly tsunami in March.\par \par \uc2 -- Japan's economy shrank much less than expected in April-June as companies made strides in restoring output after the quake. Analysts expect the economy will rebound in the third quarter although the outlook beyond that is far from certain.\par \par \uc2 ($1 = 76.565 Japanese Yen)\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Tetsushi Kajimoto, Leika Kihara, Tomasz Janowski and Kaori Kaneko; Editing by Edmund Klamann)\par \par \uc2 JAPAN-ECONOMY/TANKAN (INSTANT VIEW 2)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110818e78i0000e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
