\par\par \b \uc2 ASIAN MORNING BRIEFING: US Stocks End Lower On Grim Data; DJIA Falls 3.7%\b0\par\par\uc2 2,385 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 August 2011\par 00:12\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DJIA      10990.58  -419.63  -3.68% \par Nasdaq     2380.43  -131.05  -5.22% \par S&P 500    1140.65   -53.24  -4.46% \par FTSE 100   5092.23  -239.37  -4.49% \par Xetra DAX  5602.80  -346.14  -5.82% \par  \par 2Y Tsy  100 11/32  -0/32 0.198% +0.4BP \par 5Y Tsy  102 30/32  +3/32 0.892% -1.9BP \par 10Y Tsy 100 12/32 +24/32 2.087% -8.2BP \par  \par USD/JPY    76.55-58  -0.03% \par Range      76.70-76.46 \par EUR/USD    1.4336-40  -0.61% \par Range      1.4452-1.4272 \par AUD/USD    1.0388-92  -1.53% \par Range      1.0556-1.0356 \par  \par USD/JPY 1M Vol    10.9-11.4% \par EUR/USD 1M Vol   13.55-14.1% \par AUD/USD 1M Vol    15.7-16.75% \par  \par Comex Gold         $1,822.00  +$28.20 \par Comex Gold Range   $1,786.80-$1832.00 \par Nymex Crude (NY)      $82.38   -$5.20 \par  \par  \par U.S. SUMMARY & ASIAN OUTLOOK: \par STOCKS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Stocks tumbled amid growing fears of a global recession, as investors confronted a grim mix of U.S. economic data and fresh concerns about Europe's banks.\f28 \par \par \uc2 The Dow Jones Industrial Average ended down 419.63 points, or 3.68%, to 10990.58. The \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  500-stock index dropped 53.24 points, or 4.46%, to 1140.65, while the Nasdaq Composite lost 131.05 points, or 5.22%, to 2380.43.\par \par \uc2 In the flight to safety Thursday, investors piled into gold, which jumped to a record of $1,818.90 a troy ounce. In the Treasurys market, the yield on the benchmark 10-year note briefly dipped below 2% in intraday trading for the first time since at least 1954, as investors sought refuge in U.S. debt.\par \par \uc2 "If it's not a recession, it sure feels like one. And if it feels like one, it doesn't matter if you can prove it with statistics or not," said \uc2 John Hailer\uc2 , president and CEO of \uc2 Natixis Global Asset Management\uc2  in the U.S. and Asia.\par \par \uc2 The heaviest selling came in energy and materials stocks, as commodities prices sank. Among Dow components, United Technologies fell $3.93 a share, or 5.5%, to $68.12; \uc2 Alcoa\uc2  lost 75 cents, or 6.1%, to 11.51; and Caterpillar tumbled 4.31, or 4.9%, to 88.33. Among big oil firms, \uc2 Chevron\uc2  lost 4.44, or 4.6%, to 93.24 and \uc2 Exxon Mobil\uc2  was off 3.22, or 4.3%, to 70.94.\par \par \uc2 Bank stocks were also under significant pressure. \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  was the biggest decliner among the Dow components, tumbling 45 cents, or 6%, to 7.01, while \uc2 J.P. Morgan Chase\uc2  lost 1.38, or 3.8%, to 35.19. \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  fell 1.87, or 6.3%, to 27.98 while \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  declined 81 cents, or 4.8%, to 16.20.\par \par \uc2 "Investor nerves are raw," said John Lynch, chief investment officer for Wells Fargo Bank in the mid-Atlantic region. "A recession can become a self-fulfilling prophecy."\par \par \uc2 Hewlett-Packard\uc2 , one of the biggest stock laggards on the day, briefly reversed its morning slump after reports that the world's biggest computer maker will spin off its personal-computer business and is close to a $10 billion deal to acquire U.K. software firm Autonomy. H-P, which took the unusual step of reporting quarterly earnings during trading hours, fell 1.88, or 6%, to 29.51 despite earnings and revenue coming in largely in line with analyst expectations.\par \par \uc2 Transportation stocks, which are particularly sensitive to economic growth concerns, were hit hard. The Dow Jones Transportation Average, an index of 20 railroad, airline and shipping stocks, tumbled 6.1%. American Airlines parent AMR fell 26 cents, or 6.8%, to 3.54; \uc2 Kansas City Southern\uc2  dropped 6.49, or 12%, to 47.47; and \uc2 FedEx\uc2  lost 4.67, or 5.9%, to 74.46.\par \par \uc2 All 10 sectors of the S&P 500 were lower, with 98% of the 500 component stocks falling. The CBOE Market Volatility Index, the "fear gauge" known as the VIX, surged 37%.\par \par \uc2 Trading volumes were in line with recent trading days, which have seen a jump in activity. About 6.3 billion shares changed hands in New York Stock Exchange composite volume, well above the daily average this year of about 4.3 billion shares.\par \par \uc2 China's shares ended lower Thursday for a third consecutive session on continuing concerns about potential monetary tightening in the country as well as lingering debt woes in the U.S. and Europe.\par \par \uc2 Analysts said that the Shanghai index will face strong support at the 2526 level, the worst close last week when the index slid to its lowest level in more than a year, following \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  downgrade of U.S. debt.\par \par \uc2 "Investors may have been a bit too optimistic in hoping for domestic inflation to fall below 5% in the short term after expectations that CPI peaked in July (at 6.5%)," said Amy Lin, an analyst from Capital Securities.\par \par \uc2 "Investors earlier anticipated some policy relaxation in the current half of the year, but there's been mixed signals as to whether there may or may not be further rate hikes," said Tang Yonggang, an analyst at Hongyuan Securities, referring to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's unexpected move to raise the yield on its benchmark one-year bills Tuesday, signalling that Beijing may soon raise interest rates to cool inflation despite the turmoil in global financial markets.\par \par \uc2 Hong Kong shares ended lower Thursday, tracking weakness in Asian markets, as investors remained jittery amid worries about Europe's debt problems and a slowdown in the U.S. economy.\par \par \uc2 Brokerage Core Pacific-Yamaichi said the overall trading volume in the local market was still low, indicating that investors remain cautious. "In order to see a breakthrough, we may need to see the market pull back to create another over-sold situation," it said.\par \par \uc2 Linus Yip, a strategist at First Shanghai, said he expects the local index to trade in a 20,000-20,500 range in the near term. He said if it falls below 20,000, its Aug. 9 low of 18,868 should serve as strong support.\par \par \uc2 Tokyo stocks fell Thursday, as the Nikkei Stock Average marked a five-month closing low on investor caution about the global economic outlook, which took a particularly heavy toll on manufacturers.\par \par \uc2 "It's quite difficult for the government and the BOJ to take action now, but shares will continue to be dragged lower unless they can demonstrate their resolve to the market," said Investrust CEO Hiroyuki Fukunaga.\par \par \uc2 Crude oil futures tumbled 5.9% in U.S. trading Thursday as weakening economic data in the U.S. sparked renewed fears of a global downturn.\par \par \uc2 Those same fears pushed Comex gold futures to their third record in as many days, as investors fled sinking equities markets and turned to perceived refuge assets.\par \par \uc2 Base metals limped to a sharply lower close on the London Metal Exchange Thursday as a broad-market sell-off sent investors fleeing risky assets.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Disappointing U.S. economic data sent investors hunting for safe-haven \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Thursday, with the Swiss franc, yen and dollar all strengthening against the riskier euro.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The shift began after the Philadelphia Federal Reserve reported that regional manufacturing activity contracted in its August survey, where the market had expected a slight expansion. Adding to the anxiety, U.S. sales of previously owned homes fell in July to the lowest level this year and prices also tumbled--a sign the battered housing sector won't regain its footing any time soon.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Late Thursday, the euro was at $1.4334 from $1.4427 late Wednesday, according to EBS via CQG. The dollar was at Y76.57 from Y76.60, while the euro was at Y109.75 from Y110.50. The U.K. pound was at $1.6513 from $1.6544. The dollar was at CHF0.7941 from CHF0.7900.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The ICE Dollar Index, which tracks the U.S. dollar against a basket of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , was at 73.674 from about 74.010.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Commodity-linked \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  like the Canada, Australian and New Zealand dollars were also hit hard, as oil futures plunged 5.9% to $82.38 a barrel.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Meanwhile, a meeting of senior Japanese Finance Ministry and Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  officials kept markets on edge, as the possibility of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rises as the yen edges closer to its March low of Y76.25 against the dollar.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par BONDS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. 10-year Treasury yields plunged to historic lows Thursday, piercing below the 2% mark at one point as panicked investors brace for what seems to be a new leg downward in the global economy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Global growth and euro-zone credit problems crept up during European trading hours, and a particularly bad group of data in the U.S. session was enough to push the benchmark 10-year yields as low as 1.974%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The pace that yields are sinking at--the 10-year rate was at 2.081% Thursday from 2.747% at the beginning of the month--echoes the drop seen last summer when Greece's debt woes first flared up and U.S. investors were worried about deflation. Then last August, Federal Reserve Chairman Ben Bernanke first hinted at a second round of quantitative easing, now known as QE2.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 While rising inflation is now a hurdle to more Fed support, market participants are ratcheting up expectations that Bernanke will announce some form of monetary stimulus when he speaks at the central-banker summit at Jackson Hole, Wyo., starting Aug. 26.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The ultra-low yields, which move inversely to prices, are also giving traders an ugly reminder of the levels seen during the 2008 global financial crisis, when \f28 \uc2 \f2 Lehman Brothers\f28 \uc2 \f2 ' collapse sparked a worldwide credit freeze.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "There's a real fear out there about what's going on in Europe and the potential for a double-dip [recession]," said Michael Mata, portfolio manager of the ING \f28 \uc2 \f2 Global Bond Fund\f28 \uc2 \f2 . "It's not just in the U.S., but across the developed markets." He says the $34 billion investors pulled out of equity funds in the latest week is making its way into U.S. government paper.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A dismal Philadelphia Fed business index report Thursday sparked a wave of Treasurys buying, although some investors began booking profits, helping to stabilize 10-year yields back above 2.0%. Those notes ended the day up 24/32 to yield 2.087%--a new closing low for the year. The 30-year bond gained 2 11/32 in price, dragging yields down 12 basis points to 3.446%. Shorter-dated notes remained little changed, with the two-year yield at 0.198%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The 10-year Japanese government bond yield fell below the psychologically key 1% mark Thursday for the first time in more than a week, as continued uncertainties over the global economy prompted investors to escape from riskier assets to safer sovereign debt. [ 18-08-11 2212GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Citigroup\f28 \uc2 \f2  Global Markets Japan senior strategist Maki Shimizu expects JGBs' uptrend to continue at least until details of the ruling Democratic Party of Japan's presidential election become clearer around the end of August.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Deutsche Securities' strategist Makoto Yamashita also says domestic politics could potentially be a selling cue for the JGB market.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "If a next prime minister opposes to the idea of a reconstruction tax, it may be a trigger for a downgrade on Japan's sovereign debt ratings," which would in turn weigh on JGB prices, he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China government bonds ended slightly higher Thursday after the PBOC injected a net CNY38 billion of funds into the banking system via this week's regular open market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , a Beijing-based trader with a local brokerage. That was the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's fifth straight weekly fund injection, highlighting its efforts to ensure flush liquidity in the domestic market amid worries about fiscal woes in the U.S. and Europe, the trader said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Among data due Friday in a fairly light calendar, Taiwan export orders likely grew 11.24% on the year in July, according to the median forecast of eight economists in a Dow Jones survey, up from June's 9.18% rise. The value of July's export orders likely exceeded June's $37.36 billion on strong demand for the island's smartphones and tablet devices.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par TOP HEADLINES: \par DoJ Probes S&P's Role In Mortgage Deals \par Inflation Resumes Advance; Jobless Claims Rise \par H-P 3Q Net Up 8.6%, Aims To Buy Autonomy At GBP25.50/Share \par AIG Pays $2.15B To Treasury After Nan Shan Sale \par SEC Probes A-Power For Possible Securities Violations \par UK Police Arrest Ex-Showbiz \f28 \uc2 \f2 News Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2  Reporter \par Dudley Sees Weaker US Rebound \par Greece Says Euro-Zone Must OK Finland Deal \par Salesforce.Com Posts Loss On Write-Downs \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 LDK Solar\f28 \uc2 \f2  Cuts 2Q, 2011 Earnings Forecasts, Shares Slide \par Electric, Gas Rates To Climb Again In Oct -Nikkei \par \f28 \uc2 