\par\par \b \uc2 ASIAN MORNING BRIEFING: US Stocks Close Mixed; Retailers, Europe Debt In Focus\b0\par\par\uc2 2,642 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 August 2011\par 00:16\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DJIA      11410.21   +4.28  +0.04% \par Nasdaq     2511.48  -11.97  -0.47% \par S&P 500    1193.89   +1.13  +0.09% \par FTSE 100   5331.60  -26.03  -0.49% \par Xetra DAX  5948.94  -45.96  -0.77% \par  \par 2Y Tsy 100 11/32  -0/32 0.194% +0.7BP \par 5Y Tsy 102 28/32  +4/32 0.903% -2.6BP \par 10Y Tsy 99 22/32 +16/32 2.156% -5.8BP \par  \par USD/JPY    76.51-56  -0.36% \par Range      76.84-76.42 \par EUR/USD    1.4431-36  +0.18% \par Range      1.4516-1.4326 \par AUD/USD    1.0550-54  +0.64% \par Range      1.0600-1.0436 \par  \par USD/JPY 1M Vol    10.60-11.10% \par EUR/USD 1M Vol    12.65-13.20% \par AUD/USD 1M Vol    14.20-15.20% \par  \par Comex Gold        $1,793.80   +$8.80 \par Nymex Crude (NY)     $87.58   +$0.93 \par  \par  \par U.S. SUMMARY & ASIAN OUTLOOK: \par STOCKS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. stocks were mixed as investors weighed earnings from some high-profile retailers against signs of rising input costs and continued dissatisfaction over efforts to find a solution to Europe's debt woes.\f28 \par \par \uc2 The Dow Jones Industrial Average inched up 4.28 points, or 0.04%, to 11410.21, after gaining 124 points early in the session. The \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  500-stock index gained 1.13 points, or 0.09%, to 1193.89, while the Nasdaq Composite fell 11.97 points, or 0.47%, to 2511.48.\par \par \uc2 Pulling on the upside were stocks in the most defensive sectors: telecommunications, utilities and consumer staples. \uc2 Verizon Communications\uc2  gained 2.1%, \uc2 AT&T\uc2  added 1.3%, and \uc2 Coca-Cola\uc2  rose 1.6%.\par \par \uc2 The strength in utilities stocks "is a sign of people looking for yield and safety," added Russell Croft, co-manager of the Croft Value fund. "The 2008 and 2009 selloff is still fresh in people's heads, and a week like last week didn't make people feel any better," Croft said.\par \par \uc2 Leading the decliners were technology stocks. \uc2 Hewlett-Packard\uc2  fell 3.7% to lead the Dow laggards, after rival Dell tumbled 10%. Investors dumped shares in computer maker Dell after it reported slowing sales and lowered its full-year revenue target.\par \par \uc2 Other tech giants fell, with Cisco Systems down 0.9%, Intel off 0.6%, and \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  shedding 0.4%.\par \par \uc2 "I don't believe we're going into recession, which helps on the downside, but there's very little visibility on the upside--how are we going to get growth?" said Morris Mark, president of Mark Asset Management. "Europe may not get much worse, but it's not likely to get much better, and the failure to come up with a grand bargain in Washington leaves us with no growth policy until the election next year. When you look at growth coming out of any policy, it doesn't seem to be there."\par \par \uc2 Retail stocks were in focus Wednesday, after a batch of high-profile names reported earnings.\par \par \uc2 Target rose 2.4% after its quarterly earnings report topped estimates, driven by same-store sales growth. Staples edged up 0.5% after it reported second-quarter profit that topped forecasts. \uc2 Abercrombie & Fitch\uc2  fell 8.7% as rising costs from increased raw-material costs cut into profit margins, though fiscal-second-quarter earnings rose 64% amid rising sales at the teen-apparel retailer.\par \par \uc2 China's shares ended lower Wednesday for the second consecutive session on resurfaced inflation concerns after the banking regulator said the outlook for inflation is still uncertain as imported inflationary pressures remain high.\par \par \uc2 "What Liu (Mingkang, the chairman of China's banking regulator) says brings about uncertainties regarding views that inflation has peaked in July. Plus, lingering concerns over the fiscal health of the U.S. and European markets remain an overhang in the market," said Zhou Xu, an analyst from Nanjing Securities.\par \par \uc2 Analysts said that the Shanghai index will face resistance at 2700 for the rest of the week, due to the current lack of catalysts to drive sustainable gains.\par \par \uc2 Also, there are some concerns that Beijing may soon raise interest rates to cool inflation despite the turmoil in global financial markets, after China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  unexpectedly raised the yield on its benchmark one-year bills Tuesday for the first time since late June, analysts said.\par \par \uc2 Hong Kong shares ended higher Wednesday after a China delegation led by Vice Premier Li Keqiang announced a laundry list of measures aimed at boosting Hong Kong's economy and its role in the internationalization of yuan.\par \par \uc2 Analysts said they expect the benchmark index to trade in a range of 19,000-20,600 over the next two weeks, with the upside limited on a growing concern over the euro-zone debt woes.\par \par \uc2 "Vice Premier Li's sweeteners today (Wednesday) only managed to lift sentiment to a limited degree, as investors are more concerned about the looming problems over the state of the European economies, which will remain the key overhang of the market in the coming weeks," said Alvin Cheung, associate director of Prudential Brokerage Ltd.\par \par \uc2 Tokyo stocks fell Wednesday as computer chip- and parts makers declined after Dell lowered its full-year revenue target, while a cloudy European growth outlook weighed on broader market sentiment.\par \par \uc2 "Concerns about a slowdown in both Europe and in the United States continue to be at the core of investors' minds," said Yutaka Miura, a senior technical analyst at Mizuho Securities.\par \par \uc2 Crude oil futures prices settled higher in U.S. trading Wednesday after an industry report showed a bigger-than-expected drop in U.S. gasoline inventories.\par \par \uc2 Traders said carry-over strength from gasoline helped crude rise, even as the Energy Information Administration report was bearish for crude. The EIA reported a rise of 4.2 million barrels against expectations for a modest decline in the week ended Aug. 12. Crude was propped up too by strong buying interest ahead of the expiration of the September crude oil options contract on the \uc2 New York Mercantile Exchange\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Comex gold settled at record highs as reports that Venezuela plans to nationalize its gold industry gave futures a late-afternoon boost.\par \par \uc2 Base metals finished Wednesday firmly higher on the London Metal Exchange, as a weaker dollar and technical buying above key resistance levels conspired to push the metals into slightly higher ranges.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Swiss franc extended its gains against the euro and dollar after the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Swiss National Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  came out with a plan to stymie the franc's rise, which failed to satisfy investors who were expecting more aggressive measures.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro plunged as low as CHF1.1224 as the SNB said it would increase its liquidity measures by CHF80 billion to CHF200 billion, but didn't peg the franc to the euro as many market participants were expecting. The franc gave back some of its gains against the dollar in U.S. hours, trading at CHF0.7900 late Wednesday, from CHF0.7962 Tuesday according to CQG.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Swiss economy needs more than just \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , it needs a global economic recovery, Paul Robson, a senior \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Royal Bank of Scotland\f28 \uc2 \f2 , said. Investors are flocking to the Swiss franc as a stable asset to hold in the midst of a series of global economic scares. Tuesday's summit between French President Nicolas Sarkozy and German Chancellor Angela Merkel was the latest ripple, as the proposals to tackle Europe's sovereign debt crisis were viewed in the market as too mild.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It's too small to handle the flood of scared capital," Robson said of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  plan outlined by the SNB Wednesday. "The fortune of the franc is probably more down to Frankfurt and Paris."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Meanwhile, as the dollar fell against the franc, euro and British pound, higher commodity prices lifted growth-sensitive \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  such as the Aussie dollar, New Zealand kiwi and most European emerging \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Late Wednesday in New York, the euro was at $1.4427 from $1.4407 late Tuesday, according to EBS via CQG. The dollar was at Y76.60 from Y76.82, while the euro was at Y110.50 from Y110.68. The U.K. pound was at $1.6544 from $1.6460.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The ICE Dollar Index, which tracks the U.S. dollar against a basket of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , was unchanged at 74.010.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro tested session highs against the greenback at $1.4518 from $1.4475 following higher-than-expected U.S. producer price data, which climbed 0.2%, above the 0.1% forecast.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The market has already priced in a bearish outlook for global economic growth, says Robson. "But there are concerns that the market is talking itself into another slowdown that may or may not have been driven by supplied disruption."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Japanese yen traded uncomfortably close to its all-time low of Y76.25, while \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets continue to monitor the yen's movements to see if the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par BONDS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 An unexpected jump in U.S. producer prices Wednesday wasn't enough to knock Treasurys off their upward rally, as investors increasingly expect the Federal Reserve to introduce some form of monetary stimulus to rescue the economy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The market's attention is already focused on the Jackson Hole, Wyo., central bankers summit starting Aug. 26--the same place Fed Chairman Ben Bernanke initially signaled the second round of quantitative-easing measures one year ago.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The current global economic slowdown has investors increasingly expecting Bernanke to say the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will help out again. "The market definitely, at this stage, has expectations of something more," said Anthony Valeri, investment strategist at LPL Financial.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Longer-dated Treasurys prices climbed after choppy morning trading, ending the U.S. day near session-highs. In late Wednesday afternoon trading in New York, benchmark 10-year notes were 16/32 higher in price, pushing yields, which move inversely to prices, down to 2.156%. The 30-year bond advanced 1 20/32 to yield 3.562%, while two-year notes were unchanged in price, with the yield up 0.7 basis point at 0.194%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Instead of being scared off by an early report that showed a surprising rise in July's producer prices--a sign of inflation that would normally encourage bondholders to sell--investors remain fixated on the stalling U.S. and global economies. Inflation is regarded as a major threat for bondholders because rising prices erode the value of their stream of fixed income. [ 17-08-11 2216GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Valeri notes that real yields, as reflected in 10-year Treasury Inflation-Protected Securities, recently went negative to -0.2%. This shows the market expects no growth in the coming year. Subtracting core inflation from the current 10-year nominal yield results in a rate of about 0.6%--the lowest level since autumn 2008, when markets were embroiled in the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Lehman Brothers\f28 \uc2 \f2  downfall.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But those hoping for the Fed to step in soon also have to face the reality of rising prices. Inflation fears make it harder for the Fed to introduce more stimulus, something it didn't have to worry about last year.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The inflation readings have not been so cooperative," said Robert Tipp, chief investment strategist at Prudential Fixed Income. "The resistance to the large-scale asset purchase is very high." He says it "would require a lot of gumption" for the Fed to actually come forth with some form of stimulus plan next week, even though he believes it should.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese government bonds ended modestly higher Wednesday, following gains in U.S. Treasurys overnight due to disappointment over the results of the Germany-France summit.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "One reason why the 10-year JGB yield has established itself below 1.2% is the downward pressure created by the worsening debt crisis in Europe," said UBS Securities senior strategist Atsushi Ito. "We believe a fundamental resolution of the euro-zone's problems will require a move to a single European fiscal authority."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China government bonds ended steady Wednesday after the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sold CNY30 billion of 10-year bonds at 3.93%, nearly flat compared with the yield of 3.92% on 10-year government paper traded in the secondary market.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "As the new offering gave little trading cues, investors are now focusing on tomorrow's (Thursday's) three-month \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  bill sale," a Beijing-based analyst with a local fund said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The PBOC has kept its three-month bill yield unchanged at 3.0801% in the open market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for the past seven weeks, but there are increasing expectations it may raise the bill rate Thursday after it lifted the one-year bill yield by 8.6 basis points in Tuesday's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said late Wednesday that it would sell CNY7 billion of three-month bills and CNY1 billion of three-year bonds in Thursday's market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On the data calendar Thursday, Japan likely registered a Y70.1 billion trade surplus in July, the second straight month of surplus after June's Y68.6-billion surplus, a good sign for the nation's export-led economy, according to the median forecast of economists polled by Dow Jones and the Nikkei.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Hong Kong's unemployment rate likely held steady at 3.5% in the May-July period, unchanged from the February-April period, according to the median forecast of nine economists polled by Dow Jones Newswires.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par TOP HEADLINES: \par Sarkozy, Merkel Seek To Penalize Big-Deficit Countries \par Target Profit Rises 3.7%, Sees Year Net Above Views \par SNB Moves To Curb Swiss Franc, Boosts Liquidity \par Municipalities Abandon S&P Investment Ratings \par BoE's Dale And Weale Abandon Call For Rate Rise \par Judge OKs Transfer Of Lehman's $5.3B Loan Book \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 SabMiller\f28 \uc2 \f2  Bid For Foster's Turns Hostile \par FDA OKs Roche's Melanoma Treatment \par Obama To Offer Deficit Plan With More Cuts Than Taxes \par UAW Asks Ford Locals For Strike Authorization Vote \par Toyota Boshoku Ramps Up Auto Interior Parts Output In Europe -Nikkei \par Japan Carmakers' Global Output To Near Record In FY11 -Nikkei \par Yamaha To Hike Motorbike Output In Vietnam, India -Nikkei \par Suntory To Set Up Umbrella M&A Firm For SE Asia -Nikkei \par  \par  \par SCHEDULE OF MAIN EVENTS (times in GMT): \par 2350  Japan        Jul Provisional Trade Statistics for the Month \par 0130  Australia    Jul \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign Exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Transactions and Holdings of \par                    Official Reserve Assets \par 0130  Australia    Jul International Merchandise Imports \par 0300  S Korea      Jul Corporate Bankruptcies \par 0500  Japan        Jun Indexes of Business Conditions - Revision \par 0530  Japan        Jul Tokyo area department store sales \par 0530  Japan        Jul Nationwide department store sales \par 0630  India        Weekly WPI (for fuel products and primary \par                    articles) \par 0800  Taiwan       2Q  GDP \par 0830  Hong Kong    Jul Unemployment \par 0830  Hong Kong    Jul Underemployment \par 0830  UK           Jul CML mortgage lending \par 0830  UK           Jul Automotive production \par 0830  UK           Jul Bank of England capital issuance \par 0830  UK           Jul Retail Sales \par 0900  EU           Jun Construction output \par 1130  UK           Aug CML Market Commentary \par 1230  US           Jul CPI \par 1230  US           Aug 13 Unemployment Insurance Claims Report - Initial Claims \par 1235  US           New York Fed Pres Dudley speaks on the economic outlook in \par                    Newark, N.J. \par 1345  US           Aug 14 Bloomberg Consumer Comfort Index \par 1400  US           Aug 6 DJ-BTMU U.S. Business Barometer \par 1400  US           Aug Philadelphia Fed Business Outlook Survey \par 1400  US           Jul Leading Indicators \par 1400  US           Jul Existing Home Sales \par 1430  US           Aug 12 EIA Natural Gas Storage Report \par 2030  US           Aug 8 Money Stock Measures \par 2030  US           Aug 17