\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ: Japan Finds It Harder To Slow The Yen\b0\par\par\uc2 By Alex Frangos and Tatsuo Ito  \par \uc2 985 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 August 2011\par 19:32\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Japanese yen's near-record strength despite three government \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 -market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  over the past year is sparking debate over how effective the strategy will be in the future.\f28 \par \par \uc2 Less than two weeks since the latest Japanese government yen selling on Aug. 4, the dollar once again trades below Y77, near the Y76.25 post-World War II high for the strength of the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reached in the days after the March natural and nuclear disasters. (In yen terms, the yen stands at about $1.30 per 100 yen, up 19% from $1.10 a year earlier.)\par \par \uc2 "These \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  will experience diminishing returns," said Stephen Jen, managing partner of SLJ Macro Partners, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -focused investment firm in London. He calls the \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  "like speed bumps in Malibu," not the sort of actions that will ultimately halt the yen's ascent. "Every \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  this year has been a great opportunity for investors to re-enter the market" to buy yen, he said. "The \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been handing profits to speculators."\par \par \uc2 (This story and related background material will be available on The Wall Street Journal website, WSJ.com.)\par \par \uc2 The strong yen is putting pressure on Japan's beleaguered export sector, especially as it competes with the likes of South Korea, Taiwan and China, whose \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have risen far less quickly--and putting new pressure on Japan's Finance Ministry to pull the trigger once more.\par \par \uc2 Despite the rapid evaporation of the effects from this month's moves, Japanese government officials continue to hint they are poised to act again. "Since unstable market conditions continue, we will watch the market closely and take decisive steps if necessary," Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda said on a Sunday TV program. As the leading candidate to succeed Prime Minister Naoto Kan, who is expected to step down at the end of the month, Noda has political and economic incentives to appear decisive on the matter.\par \par \uc2 Officials won't talk publicly about their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy, but one anticipated trigger for a new round of dollar-buying would be if the greenback breaks through the previous low. Japanese authorities are nervously eyeing next week's speech by Ben Bernanke at the U.S. Federal Reserve's annual Jackson Hole conference in Wyoming. If the Fed chief signals a further easing of monetary policy, Japanese officials are braced for a further tumbling of the dollar.\par \par \uc2 A second foray this month by the Finance Ministry would break the recent pattern of limited \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and would appear to be a step closer to the more-aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy taken by the Japanese government through the 1990s and early 2000s.\par \par \uc2 From January 2003 to March 2004, for instance, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  conducted 138 \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , spending Y35 trillion ($456 billion), according to \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  data. From March 2004 until September 2010, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  didn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  at all. The Finance Ministry then conducted a one-day \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  last September, another in conjunction with the Group of Seven this past March, and one more on Aug. 4.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  has sufficient budget for more \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 . According to the ministry's data, it had room to spend about Y40 trillion as of March. Of this, it appeared to have used Y4.6 trillion for the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Aug. 4, the biggest ever for single-day action. The government can boost the ceiling if needed when it compiles the third extra budget for earthquake reconstruction later this year.\par \par \uc2 "You should definitely take them seriously when they talk about further action," said Sean Callow, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist for \uc2 Westpac Banking\uc2  in Sydney. "Historically isolated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is the exception."\par \par \uc2 While the rise of the yen back to highs against the dollar would make it seem that the \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  were failures, Callow noted that it is impossible to know if that really is true. "Given what happened in the world in the past week or so, a modest decline in the dollar versus the yen isn't so bad. We never will know what would have happened without that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he said.\par \par \uc2 "The resolve is strong," said Christopher Brandon, portfolio manager at Rhicon \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Management in Singapore. He said \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is "getting smarter about the way they \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ; picking the right time to do it. They are doing a better job of getting insight when the market is built up of positions and catching the market off guard." That has pushed his technically driven fund to "somewhat diminish" activity in the yen space.\par \par \uc2 But Japanese officials don't seem inclined to return all the way to the rapid fire 2003-04 strategy. They anticipate a constant campaign would draw a backlash from the struggling U.S. and European economies, who might complain if Japan is seen as trying to export its way out of a downturn.\par \par \uc2 If a Bernanke speech, or Fed action, triggers another dollar slump, Finance Ministry officials believe that their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be more successful if the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which operates independently from the government, tried to offset the Fed's moves--accompanying any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  with further monetary easing. That is, it would be essentially increasing the supply of yen to cheapen its value.\par \par \uc2 Some in the government hope the Bank of Japan becomes bolder on credit easing.\par \par \uc2 Economy Minister Kaoru Yosano, who is normally seen as a supporter of the Bank of Japan making decisions without government pressure, recently uncharacteristically referred to a need for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to consider a further credit easing.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, officials involved in the Finance Ministry's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy are cutting short vacations, even during the traditional August holiday period. "I won't be able to take a long holiday this year...a half-day off at the longest," one official said.\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 August 16, 2011 13:32 ET (17:32 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020110816e78g005h2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
