\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ BLOG/ \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  Real Time:  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  101: The BOJ Rate Check\b0\par\par\uc2 847 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 August 2011\par 13:22\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 (This story has been posted on The Wall Street Journal Online's Japan Real Time Report blog at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://blogs.wsj.com/japanrealtime"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://blogs.wsj.com/japanrealtime }}}\uc2 .)\par \par \uc2 By Takashi Mochizuki\par \par \uc2 When the dollar strengthens by a full yen in the space of seconds, Tokyo \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders know something's up. But it's not automatically full-blown yen \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Take the late afternoon here Thursday, when the greenback jumped to Y77.30 from Y76.30. With the rumor mill moving into overdrive within instants, savvy traders nailed it at lightning speed. The jump wasn't because of actual \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , it was more than likely a rumor about what the trade knows as a "rate check" by the Bank of Japan.\par \par \uc2 A rate check?\par \par \uc2 When the government decides it's time to give \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers a barely coded, not-so-gentle reminder that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , using the BOJ as its agent, is all set to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  any time it likes, it does so by having officials call market people to inquire about rates -- hence the "rate check." Some say the rate check is a kind of verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but far more effective than government ministers verbally expressing concern about \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in media appearances, because dealers believe it's a warning the market is just one step away from actual \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , or BOJ, and commercial banks, both Japanese and non-Japanese, communicate with each other regularly. Every day, in fact. Almost around the same time every day, the BOJ makes calls to banks, asking for a quick briefing on what happened in the overnight London and New York markets. When markets are jittery about the possibility of imminent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- like now -- some market speculators love to call this regular communication a rate-check, spreading rumor in the hope of manipulating the market.\par \par \uc2 But be aware -- that is not your actual warning rate check.\par \par \uc2 If the BOJ makes a call to banks at an unusual time, or when the market is moving sharply, that's the rate check. And it's important to find out just exactly how the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  checked rates, because the rate check comes with several shades of warning.\par \par \uc2 Here's how it goes.\par \par \uc2 A mild warning: "Why the yen is rising so sharply?" The question suggests the authorities are concerned about price moves, and possibly gathering information as preparation work before \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Naturally, the authorities are of course already extremely aware of market conditions, such as where orders are, and how big they are, before ever considering the market.\par \par \uc2 A heavy hint: "Where's dollar-yen?" The BOJ has many data terminals in its offices -- the bank doesn't need to ask dealers to know the levels. By asking this obvious question to dealers, the BOJ is showing its readiness to place an order, dealers say. This is the most frequently observed type of the rate-check.\par \par \uc2 Heavy enough to dent the dealing room floor: "How much is $10?" (the number isn't the same every time.) This effectively means, "I want to buy so please let me know the price." In the world of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers, $10 would stand for $10 million, and the question is regularly asked this way by traders transacting \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- and by the BOJ placing \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  orders. When asked, a counterpart provides a yen amount that buys the number of dollars proposed. All a buyer needs to do to make a deal is say, "Mine."\par \par \uc2 When bank dealers receive a question like this from the BOJ, they will be 99.999% sure that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is just about to place an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  order. But if the response to a dealer's yen offer is not, "Mine," but rather, "Nothing," the dealer has just been rate-checked.\par \par \uc2 Traders say checking rates this way is actually rude, and anyone who utters the dreaded "Nothing" to offers will be disliked. "It's like at a clothing store, you show off your gold credit card, do a trial fitting and leave without buying anything," said one dealer.\par \par \uc2 And of course, when word spreads that the BOJ is dropping the heaviest hint of all that it's checking rates, there's no time to stand on ceremony. Dealers at banks on the receiving end of the questions will then make separate, big deals for themselves instantly to try to cash in on what could be the imminent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . And in the end, the current rate moves, hence the speculator's interest in convincing the market the BOJ is on a rate-check mission.\par \par \uc2 Of course, a BOJ rate-check rumor is difficult to confirm: The BOJ would never comment on whether it checked rates, and no written documents, unlike actual \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , that might confirm it are ever released. Another reason why a rate-check rumor is so popular among speculators. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  -For continuously updated news from The Wall Street Journal, see WSJ.com at  \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://wsj.com }}}\uc2 \f2 . \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 August 12, 2011 07:22 ET (11:22 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020110812e78c0037s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
