\par\par \b \uc2 BOJ Shirakawa: No change to dollar's key  \highlight22\uc2 fx \highlight\uc2  status\b0\par\par\uc2 320 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 August 2011\par 00:46\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Aug 13 (Reuters) - Bank of Japan Governor Masaaki Shirakawa said there was no immediate change to the dollar's status as a key global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  despite \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  downgrade of the U.S. sovereign debt rating, a Japanese newspaper reported on Saturday.\par \par \uc2 "It takes a very long time for the world's key \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to change, as was the case when it shifted to the U.S. dollar from the British pound," Shirakawa said in an interview with the Mainichi paper conducted on Friday and published on Saturday.\par \par \uc2 "The U.S. economy is the world's biggest and the market for dollars is the world's most liquid. Everyone uses (the dollar) and I don't think the current situation will change immediately," he said.\par \par \uc2 The dollar has fallen broadly, particularly against safe haven \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  like the yen and the Swiss franc, on S&P's downgrade of the U.S. credit rating last week and worries about the health of the U.S. economy.\par \par \uc2 Shirakawa also said the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's monetary easing last week was a pre-emptive move to counter the potential harm recent yen rises could have on Japan's economy, and that he preferred to assess its effect on the economy for now.\par \par \uc2 But he also reiterated that the BOJ will take appropriate action based on economic conditions at the time, signalling the bank's readiness to ease policy again if further yen rises hurt \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's growth prospects, the paper said.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and the BOJ eased policy last week in an effort to stem the yen's rise -- which had taken the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  near record highs -- from derailing Japan's recovery from the devastation of the March earthquake and tsunami.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Leika Kihara, Editing by Chizu Nomiyama)\par \par \uc2 JAPAN-ECONOMY/BOJ   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110815e78c001bx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
