\par\par \b \uc2 WORLD  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 : Dollar Maintains Firm Tone, Euro Weakens On Sluggish Stocks, Bank Worries\b0\par\par\uc2 612 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 August 2011\par 07:34\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 -- Dollar retains firm tone in Asian trade\par \par \uc2 -- Euro weakens as Wall Street rally fails to excite other equity markets\par \par \uc2 -- Trading volumes cut during Japan summer holiday period\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Kosaku Narioka \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--The dollar maintained a generally firm tone in Asian trading while the euro trended lower Friday, with Japan's summer holiday period cutting into volume.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar held onto gains overnight in New York, treading water around Y76.85 in what would appear to be--at least temporarily--out of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  range for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . The dollar slid Thursday to Y76.30, provoking a rate check by the Bank of Japan that pushed the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  back up.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Asked whether last week's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  had been a success, Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda said, "At this stage it is still too early to tell."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Aug. 4 \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's largest ever, judging by data released by the Bank of Japan, at around Y4.6 trillion.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Noda said Friday the government will consider various measures if the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's "one-sided" moves continue.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar was at Y76.80 as of 0450 GMT, from Y76.83 late in New York trade Thursday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro trended lower after, meanwhile, Asian stock markets failed to get swept up by Wall Street's strong gains overnight, with the Dow Jones industrial average up 3.95%. As of 0500 GMT, the Nikkei Stock Average was down 0.6% at 8931.24.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Some traders said moves by European regulators to ban short-selling of financial stocks had created fresh concerns about the underlying situation.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Tomohiro Nishida, senior dealer at Chuo Mitsui Trust and Banking, said these are moves "they wouldn't do under ordinary circumstances."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro was at $1.4183, from $1.4241 late Thursday in New York, and at Y108.87 from Y109.29.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The market was subdued with smaller-than-normal trading volumes, especially in Tokyo where the annual late-summer holiday had begun.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Among other \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the U.K. pound was at $1.6198 from $1.6230. The dollar was at CHF0.7600 from CHF0.7620.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The ICE Dollar Index was at 74.779 from 74.636.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Looking ahead, second-quarter GDP figures for France and European Union industrial production figures for June are due to be released.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Among U.S. data to watch are retail sales figures for July and the University of Michigan consumer sentiment index for August.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Interbank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign Exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Rates At 23:50 EDT / 0450 GMT \par  \par                          Latest     Previous   %Chg   Daily   Daily    %Chg \par                                     2150 GMT          High    Low      12/31 \par USD/JPY Japan            76.78-80       76.82-86  -0.07    77.02    76.78  -