\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 760 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 August 2011\par 04:18\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par JAPAN GOVT PROJECTS FISCAL 2011 GDP GROWTH OF 0.5% \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese Prime Minister Naoto Kan's Cabinet approved Friday a medium-term fiscal framework and growth projections based on an expected Y19 trillion in spending on reconstruction from the March 11 earthquake and tsunami. (published 0049 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par S KOREA FINANCE MINISTER: INFLATION CONDITIONS REMAIN DIFFICULT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The South Korean government will continue with its efforts to stabilize inflation amid heightened internal and external price uncertainties, Finance Minister Bahk Jae-wan said Friday. (published 0127 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par S KOREA IMPORT PRICES DOWN FOR THIRD STRAIGHT MONTH IN JULY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korea's import prices fell for the third straight month in July because of an appreciating won and lower-priced agricultural and fishery products, which eased growing inflation pressures. (published 2100 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par FORMER CHINA LAWMAKER: NARROWER TRADE SURPLUS COULD SLOW \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  RESERVES GROWTH \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A narrower trade surplus would prevent China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves from growing too fast, and would also help avoid trade conflicts, a former official of China's legislature said Friday. (published 0210 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CHINA WILL LET YUAN RISE FURTHER - REPORT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China will bring the yuan's exchange rate to the fore to contain inflation at a time when the global economic recovery is faltering, a commentary run by the state-run China Securities Journal said Friday. (published 0002 GMT)\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 JAPAN\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  FIN MIN NODA: PREMATURE TO ASSESS EFFECTS OF \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENTION\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  LAST WEEK \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's Finance Minister said Friday it would be premature to assess the effects that last week's yen-selling \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  have had on the market. (published 0143 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par BANK INDONESIA OFFICIAL: \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  RESERVES $122.7B WED VS $123.2B LAST FRIDAY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bank Indonesia's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves fell slightly to $122.7 billion as of Wednesday from $123.2 billion as of last Friday partly due to repayment of the government's offshore debt, Deputy Governor Hartadi Sarwono said Friday. (published 0201 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par VENEZUELA FINALIZING NEW LOANS TOTALING $8B WITH CHINA, RUSSIA \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Venezuela is finalizing agreements for two separate credit lines of $4 billion each with Russia and China, with a portion of the financing earmarked for military equipment for the South American nation, according to Venezuelan state media. (published 2207 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par WSJ UPDATE: REGULATORS IN EUROPE MOVE TO BAN SHORT SELLING \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 National regulators in Spain, Italy, Belgium and France will impose new bans on short selling or on short positions as of Aug. 12, the European Securities and Markets Authority said in a statement late Thursday. (published 2202 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par WSJ: ECB NOTES 'CLOSE CALL' ON INFLATION \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 It is a "close call" whether the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will pull back on its warning against inflation, a top official said in an interview, suggesting sharply weaker economic growth and falling oil prices are spurring the bank to rethink its view of the greatest risks to the economy. (published 2252 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par OBAMA: EUROPEAN DEBT CRISIS 'LAPPING UP' ON US SHORES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 President Barack Obama on Thursday said the European debt crisis is "lapping up" on U.S. shores, and said a recent downgrade of the U.S. debt was avoidable if Congress worked together to solve the country's fiscal woes. (published 1932 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par ROUBINI: "KARL MARX WAS RIGHT, AT SOME POINT CAPITALISM CAN DESTROY ITSELF" \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Economist Nouriel Roubini, in an interview with The Wall Street Journal Thursday, reached back into history and paraphrased another well-known economist, to best explain what he thought of today's roiled global economy. (published 2140 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par     ======= DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ======= \par  \par  \par HEARD ON THE STREET: EUROPE SELLS ITSELF SHORT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Now it really does feel like 2008. Late Thursday, European market authorities announced they would initiate or extend bans on "short selling" of certain financial stocks in France, Italy, Spain and Belgium. (published 2229 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par WSJ SURVEY: SOME ECONOMISTS DOUBT FED TO STICK TO RATE DATE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Though the Federal Reserve explicitly said earlier this week that interest rates will likely stay at exceptionally low levels until mid-2013, about 1 in 3 economists expect the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to act before then. (published 2000 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par WSJ: DID DOWNGRADE IGNORE U.S.'S GREATER DEBT SUSTAINABILITY? \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Rather like the hapless Eccles, investment funds have got to be somewhere. In the absence of lucrative investment opportunities on Mars or some other extra-terrestrial destination, the money has to stay on Earth. As the tail of the financial dragon that was born in 2008 scythes down more triple-A rated borrowers, investors are left with fewer and fewer unassailable credits. (published 2008 GMT) [ 12-08-11 0218GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110812e78c0004l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
