\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  WEEK AHEAD: Euro Faces Test As German-French Leaders Meet\b0\par\par\uc2 By Tania Chen   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 831 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 August 2011\par 23:13\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global FX & Fixed Income News\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 -Markets are anxiously awaiting the results of next week's Franco-German summit\par \par \uc2 -Euro remains resilient despite ongoing blows from the euro zone and contagion permeating to France\par \par \uc2 -\uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  could decide on action to weaken the CHF\par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--The euro endured a gut-wrenching trading week without breaking free of its recent range. But holding the line is going to get more and more challenging the longer that the central questions of the euro-zone debt crisis, and the Swiss franc's rise, go unaddressed.\par \par \uc2 Analysts say the euro's outlook is skewed downward despite its recent resilience, because economic and financial conditions in the euro zone continue to deteriorate at a faster pace than expected. Meanwhile, the perceived lack of leadership in confronting the sovereign debt crisis is creating widespread market anxiety.\par \par \uc2 "They need to stop messing around with slingshots and get the bazookas out [to] deal more decisively with debt problems," said Dean Junkans, chief investment officer of Wells Fargo Private Bank, which manages $210 billion in assets.\par \par \uc2 The euro continues to trade in a narrow range against the dollar late Friday at $1.4250.\par \par \uc2 Next week's Franco-German summit could change the tide of skittish sentiment, or it could reinforce investors' fears. Markets have high expectations that German Chancellor Angela Merkel and French President Nicolas Sarklozy could come up with some cohesive plan on their own to support Italy and Spain--the two sovereigns posing the biggest threat to the euro zone right now--even though both leaders have shown resistance to euro-wide proposals such as a multi-country Eurobond to finance bailouts.\par \par \uc2 The German-French meeting is "suggestive of a new initiative," says \uc2 Brown Brothers Harriman\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Marc Chandler. But he added that if they fail to deliver that, "they risk adding to the market turmoil. Increasing the size of the EFSF [European Financial Stability Fund], agreeing on a European bond are interesting possibilities. But something bolder would be better."\par \par \uc2 The problem is that one of those two summit leaders' countries now finds itself in traders' crosshairs. Rampant speculation that France could be the next euro-zone country to be downgraded after \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  stripped the U.S. of its triple-A rating last week has fueled fear of contagion in debt markets, despite the major ratings firms stating that their ratings for the country remain unchanged. Weaker-than-expected GDP data out of France, coupled with plummeting share prices for French banks, have ensured that investors remain wary of holding French debt.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, fears continue to grow over Spanish and Italian banks and over the prospect of a Greek sovereign debt default, to which French banks are especially exposed.\par \par \uc2 For now, the solutions look like stop-gap measures. The selloff in euro zone markets prompted several European nations to impose a two-week ban on short-selling of financial stocks. Meanwhile, Nomura Securities estimates that the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has purchased almost EUR25 billion in Italian and Spanish debt.\par \par \uc2 Many investors challenged the efficacy of such measures and argued for bolder measures by the euro zone governments. Well Fargo's Junkans, for one, wants the EFSF bailout fund increased to EUR1 trillion from its current $440 billion.\par \par \uc2 As the euro-zone concerns have risen in recent months, the Swiss franc has soared as a safe haven from those risks. It has also been bolstered by a deteriorating outlook for the U.S. economy--confimed Friday by the lowest reading since 1980 for a key consumer sentiment measure--and the Federal Reserve's pledge on Tuesday to keep rates near zero for two more years, both of which work against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 Now, traders are positioning for the risk that the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  takes decisive action to weaken its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , after an SNB official suggested on Thursday that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would consider the drastic step of pegging the franc to the euro at a lower level. In response, the Swiss \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  plunged 5% against the euro.\par \par \uc2 Jens Nordvig, head of G10 \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at Nomura Securities, has his doubts. Although it's clear that the SNB is trying "to show that they are exploring extreme options," it will likely let the franc recover on its own, he said, adding that this will likely happen before the franc reaches parity versus the euro.\par \par \uc2 The euro and dollar were up 2% against the franc Friday afternoon.\par \par \uc2 And if all that's not enough, eyes are also on the Japanese yen, which has rebounded to come close to record highs after \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken it a week ago. If it goes higher, many believe authorities might act again.\par \par \uc2 The dollar was weaker against the yen at Y76.72.\par \par \uc2 -By Tania Chen, Dow Jones Newswires; 212-416-7297; tania.chen@dowjones.com [ 08-12-11 1713ET ]\par \par \uc2 1141   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110812e78c0004y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picsc