\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ/The Source:  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Duel Doomed To Fail\b0\par\par\uc2 404 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 August 2011\par 13:19\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Posted by Katie Martin \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan and Switzerland need to stop fighting unwinnable battles on their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , and start coming up with some seriously clever ideas on the global economy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 Both are lumped with a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  that investors want to buy when the going gets tough. (And the going is seriously tough right now.) So the yen is hovering around its strongest levels since World War II, while the Swissie looks like it could realistically hit parity against the euro--a truly laughable idea just a couple of weeks ago.\par \par \uc2 Both countries want to protect exporters and try and avoid a spiral of deflation caused by super-cheap imports.\par \par \uc2 But every time one of them whacks its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the other suffers.\par \par \uc2 Wednesday, the Swiss took the initiative, with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  churning billions of francs' worth of extra liquidity into the system in an effort to tame its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 --the second such move in two weeks.\par \par \uc2 By Thursday, this was starting to have some effect, with the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  edging lower, fueled in addition by suspicion among traders that yet more franc-weakening measures could be just around the corner.\par \par \uc2 Problem is, while the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  can deflect some franc buyers, it simply cannot reverse the rush to safety, unless it has any clever ideas up its sleeve over how to solve the financial crisis. (SNB: we're all ears.) It's worth noting that gold trades close to $1,800/oz.\par \par \uc2 So as the Swissie buying slowed, the yen buying accelerated. Cue a fresh push higher in the yen towards de-facto all-time highs, prompting the Bank of Japan to sniff around traders' yen rates Thursday. Super-nervous traders took that as a cue to hammer the yen.\par \par \uc2 This wasn't a "proper" \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  didn't sell any yen on the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 's behalf. So the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  quickly jumped back to where it started. Still, the BOJ has sent a message, so the Swiss had better brace themselves for a new wave of Swissie buying.\par \par \uc2 The phrase "circular firing squad" springs to mind.\par \par \uc2 Time for a plan B, or whatever letter we're up to now. Thinking caps on, please.\par \par \uc2 -For continuously updated news from The Wall Street Journal, see WSJ.com at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://wsj.com }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 August 11, 2011 07:19 ET (11:19 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020110811e78b003ls\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
