\par\par \b \uc2 Yen Plunges; Official Action Not Suspected\b0\par\par\uc2 172 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 August 2011\par 10:10\par \uc2 Dow Jones Business News\par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 LONDON -(Dow Jones)- The yen fell sharply against the dollar and euro in volatile trading Thursday before snapping back, with traders saying there was no sign the Bank of Japan was behind the sharp moves.\par \par \uc2 In a sudden plunge, the yen weakened to Y77.30 against the dollar before recovering to trade at Y76.700 by 0737 GMT. Similarly, against the euro the yen fell to Y109.912 before reversing the move quickly to trade at Y109.15 by 0739 GMT.\par \par \uc2 Traders said the Bank of Japan wasn't selling the yen, but they also said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  conducted a \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate check Thursday, sparking the yen slide.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market last Thursday as concerns over global growth and the euro-zone sovereign debt crisis left \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  investors running to the cover of the safe-haven \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 -By Clare Connaghan, Dow Jones Newswires; +44 (0) 20 7842 9496, clare.connaghan@dowjones.com [ 08-11-11 0410ET ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJON000020110811e78b0001h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
