\par\par \b \uc2 ASIAN MORNING BRIEFING: US Stocks Surge As Favorable Jobless Data, Cisco Boost\b0\par\par\uc2 2,488 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 August 2011\par 00:11\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 Thai markets are closed Friday for the Queen's birthday.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DJIA      11143.31  +423.37  +3.95% \par Nasdaq     2492.68  +111.63  +4.69% \par S&P 500    1172.64   +51.88  +4.63% \par FTSE 100   5162.83  +155.67  +3.11% \par Xetra DAX  5797.66  +184.24  +3.28% \par  \par 2Y Tsy 100 12/32   -0/32 0.184%  +0.4BP \par 5Y Tsy 102 11/32  -13/32 1.009%  +8.0BP \par 10Y Tsy 98 3/32 -1 12/32 2.333% +15.2BP \par  \par USD/JPY    76.84-88  -0.01% \par Range      77.30-76.32 \par EUR/USD    1.4224-26  +0.34% \par Range      1.4293-1.4104 \par AUD/USD    1.0344-46  +1.62% \par Range      1.0358-1.0114 \par  \par Comex Gold        $1,751.50   -$32.80 \par Comex Gold Range  $1,734.50-$1,817.60 \par Nymex Crude (NY)     $85.72    +$2.83 \par  \par  \par U.S. SUMMARY & ASIAN OUTLOOK: \par STOCKS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 U.S. stocks notched a massive advance Thursday, as investors seized on favorable corporate and economic reports to recover most of the ground they lost in the previous session.\par \par \uc2 The blue-chip Dow Jones Industrial Average soared 423.37 points, or 3.95%, to 11143.31, the second biggest point and percentage gain this year. It was the fourth straight move of 400 points or greater, a first in the Dow's history.\par \par \uc2 The action followed a 519.83-point slump Wednesday that was the blue-chip index's ninth-largest point drop ever, reflecting mounting worries about the health of Europe's banks and the chances of a global economic recession.\par \par \uc2 Thursday's two big catalysts were a rare favorable weekly employment report and signs of a turnaround at networking-equipment maker Cisco Systems. The absence of troublesome new developments in European sovereign debt and banking also helped. Markets have swung violently this week and last as investors weighed the chances of a global economic recession, and as they fretted over further debt turmoil on two continents.\par \par \uc2 The huge swing to the upside relieved some investors, but to others it was more a symptom of the extreme volatility that has gripped the markets.\par \par \uc2 "It's great to have these up days, but I think that we are going to continue to be in a choppy period with these big swings," said Margaret Patel, senior portfolio manager at \uc2 Wells Capital Management\uc2 . "The reason we had our meltdown was really over in Europe. It made the markets look like one of the dark days in 2008, melting away with no rhyme or reason."\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  500-stock index jumped 51.88 points, or 4.63%, to 1172.64, led by battered financial stocks. The Nasdaq Composite surged 111.63 points, or 4.69%, to 2492.68. Like the Dow, both indexes' gains were the second largest all year in point and percentage terms.\par \par \uc2 Cisco Systems was the strongest blue-chip stock, surging $2.19, or 16%, to 15.92, after a late Wednesday earnings report highlighted by stronger-than-expected revenues.\par \par \uc2 The number of people claiming new jobless benefits fell last week to the lowest level in four months, dropping to a seasonally adjusted 395,000 in the week ended Aug. 6.\par \par \uc2 Bank of America\uc2 , which slumped 11% Wednesday, was the second strongest Dow component behind Cisco. The bank's stock rose 48 cents, or 7.1%, to 7.25.\par \par \uc2 In corporate news, AOL gained 1.25, or 12%, to 11.47, after announcing a $250 million stock-repurchase program. \uc2 Advance Auto Parts\uc2  climbed 4.19, or 8.4%, to 54.29, after besting earnings estimates and upping its share repurchase authorization.\par \par \uc2 China's shares ended higher Thursday, boosted by a media report that the national pension fund has bought at least CNY10 billion of shares to support the market.\par \par \uc2 Despite the rise, trading volume remained thin, reflecting investor caution amid the struggles of many Western economies, analysts said.\par \par \uc2 "I feel there's an invisible hand behind the market," said Xu Yinhui, an analyst at Guotai Junan Securities. "I don't see retail investors and fund managers putting money into the market."\par \par \uc2 He said most investors are staying on the sidelines after the recent heavy selloff due to uncertainty about China's monetary policy outlook and concerns about a global recession.\par \par \uc2 "If the (Economic Observer) report is true, CNY10 billion is far from enough to support the market. It's a drop in the bucket," said Wu Dazhong, an analyst at Shenyin Wanguo Securities.\par \par \uc2 Hong Kong's benchmark stock index fell Thursday, with \uc2 HSBC\uc2  and Tencent leading the fall, tracking Wall Street's steep overnight declines because of fresh concerns about Europe's sovereign debt troubles.\par \par \uc2 Ben Kwong, associate director of \uc2 KGI Asia Ltd\uc2 ., said he expects the local index to trade between 19,000 and 20,000 in the near term.\par \par \uc2 "The fundamental problem is the real economies (globally) haven't really recovered in the past two years, it was only the financial markets that had a recovery," he said.\par \par \uc2 Tokyo stocks fell Thursday, as another steep sell-off in overnight markets and renewed concerns about European debt problems took a toll on insurers and investors sought safer haven in domestic demand-related shares.\par \par \uc2 "Investors are shifting their money into domestic areas like food, apparel and paper; they are not getting out of equities altogether because so far, Japan is still on-course for a second-half recovery and many stocks look undervalued with price-to-book ratios under 1.0X," said an analyst at a Japanese brokerage.\par \par \uc2 Oil futures shot to their highest level all week in U.S. trading Thursday, powered by a stock-market rally and an upbeat report on the U.S. labor market.\par \par \uc2 Gold prices buckled under pressure from anxious investors who cashed in recent gains in a bid to keep their wealth safe, but losses were limited as traders kept a weary eye on Europe's sovereign debt problems.\par \par \uc2 The price of copper bounced back toward $9,000 a metric ton on the London Metal Exchange Thursday, as base metals also benefited from the better-than-expected U.S. labor market report.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro strengthened Thursday as negative euro zone headlines were unable to dent risk sentiment and the rally in the U.S. equities markets provided a boost.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Early in the day, the Swiss franc plunged against the euro and the dollar amid rampant speculation that Switzerland would resort to dramatic measures to weaken its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Some even suggested Switzerland could take the drastic step of pegging its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to the euro, which itself has been hurt by Europe's sovereign debt crisis.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "By pegging the franc to the euro, Switzerland would repeat the mistake that China has made by pegging the Yuan to the dollar," said Peter Schiff, chief executive officer of brokerage firm Euro Pacific Capital. "Such a move would add to global imbalances, deny investors an important safe haven \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , and place an unfair tax on the Swiss people who will no longer enjoy the benefits of a strong \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As the day progressed, the rally in the U.S. stock markets took center stage as the Dow gained nearly 500 points.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 For Friday, market participants are pegging hopes on stronger data to keep the mood buoyant. July's retail sales figures and the University of Michigan's consumer sentiment data will be posted, providing some indicators for the U.S. economy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It is difficult to gauge swings in the risk complex at this time and on Friday, we could extend this move if the data are positive," said Dan Katzive, director in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategy research team at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Credit Suisse\f28 \uc2 \f2  in New York. "The market has not shown a lot of persistence. Market sentiment is very sensitive to headline risk and data so it could be hurt by adverse headlines."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Late Thursday in New York, the euro was at $1.4241 from $1.4178 late Wednesday, according to EBS via CQG. The dollar was at Y76.83 from Y76.86, while the euro was at Y109.29 from Y108.966. The U.K. pound was at $1.6230 from $1.6130. The dollar was at CHF0.7620 from CHF0.72660.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The ICE Dollar Index, which tracks the U.S. dollar against a basket of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , was at 74.636, unchanged on the day.\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets are also waiting to see if the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and some expect a fresh round of yen selling by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  soon.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The BOJ last week spent around $52 billion buying dollars against the yen in an effort to cap the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's appreciation, but the move failed to have a lasting impact as global economic growth concerns and heightened fiscal worries in the U.S. and Europe spurred investors to seek shelter in safe-haven \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  such as the yen.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par BONDS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A two-month streak of solid Treasury auctions hit a wall Thursday as a broad distaste for low-yielding long-dated bonds forced primary dealers to buy a major bulk of the afternoon's 30-year bond sale.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The $16 billion auction had the lowest level of indirect bidders--a gauge of foreign interest--since February 2008, hinting at both a distaste for ultra-low yields and central banks' reservations about locking themselves into longer-term U.S. government debt. The hesitance toward buying long bonds was reflected in indirect bidders taking just 12.2% of the sale. That is significantly lower than the 39.1% recent average. Primary dealers--a group of buyers that are required to take any leftover securities--had to purchase nearly half the offering.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The poor overall results come in stark contrast to the well-bid three- and 10-year sales Tuesday and Wednesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Though investors remain wary of the economic outlook, Thursday's auction dropped off because they didn't want to be stuck holding such low-yielding paper for a long time. The 30-year bond was offering just 3.60% heading into the auction, the lowest level since last September.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It was quite horrific," said John Canavan, fixed-income analyst at Stone & McCarthy. "Not only were indirect bids nearly non-existent, but dealer bidding wasn't anything special either." Canavan said the pre-auction yields were "far too low" by historical standards to garner significant interest. [ 11-08-11 2211GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Treasury prices stuck around session lows in late Thursday afternoon trading, with 30-year bonds leading the sell-off. They declined 4 28/32 in price, pushing yields up nearly 25 basis points to 3.784%--the largest leap since July 1996. Benchmark 10-year notes followed suit, falling 1 12/32 in price to yield 2.333%. Shorter-dated notes remain well-anchored as a result of the Federal Reserve committing to keep interest rates lows through at least mid-2013. Two-year notes were just a fraction lower in price to yield 0.184%. The session was the first decisive sell-off this week.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China government bonds ended higher Thursday after the PBOC injected a net CNY70 billion into the money market this week through its open-market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , its largest weekly fund injection since late June and aimed at keeping liquidity flush in the domestic market amid the global market turmoil.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "As China's inflation rate is widely expected to have peaked in July and the current global economic environment is very complicated, there is little chance the policymakers would launch further tightening measures in the near term," a Beijing-based trader with a local insurer said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The lead September Japanese government bond futures fell Thursday as an auction of five-year JGBs failed to meet strong demand.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Looking ahead, the overall trend of the market remains favorable to safe-haven assets, said Mizuho Securities market analyst Naoki Tsuchiyama, because of the strong yen and weak shares.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investors will pay attention to Japan's revision of consumer price index data using the new base year of 2010, said RBS Securities bond strategist RuiXue Xu. The government will release revised CPI figures for January to June at 0800 GMT Friday, and the change is expected to push the index sharply lower.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "If our expectations that the CPI may be revised sharply lower turn out to be correct, investor expectations for additional BOJ easing will increase" Xu said, fueling more bond-buying.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Among other data due Friday, Hong Kong's second-quarter gross domestic product likely rose 6.0% on the year, slowing from the first-quarter's 7.2% rise, according to the median forecast from nine economists polled by Dow Jones. On the quarter, GDP likely rose 0.7%, down from a rise of 2.8% in the first quarter, according to the poll.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 India's industrial output growth likely remained modest in June amid a tightening monetary policy aimed at controlling inflation in Asia's third-largest economy. June factory output likely rose 5.5% from a year earlier, compared with May's 5.6% increase, according to the median estimate in a Dow Jones poll of 17 economists.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par TOP HEADLINES: \par US Weekly Jobless Claims Dip; Trade Gap Expands \par France Bans Short Selling On Certain Financial Stocks \par ESMA: Belgium, France, Italy, Spain Set New Bans On Short Positions \par Final Debt-Panel Members Named \par USDA Cuts Corn, Wheat And Soy Production Views \par French Bank Stocks Rise In Volatile Trading \par Swiss Franc Plunges On Possible SNB Policy Moves \par Citadel Weighs Exit From Investment Banking \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Wal-mart\f28 \uc2 \f2  Exploring Acquisition Of Carrefour's Brazilian Unit \par FSA Will Not Impose Short-Selling Ban On UK Stocks \par S Korea Import Prices Down For Third Straight Month In July \par Nippon Gas Expanding Into Retail Energy Markets In Southeast Asia -Nikkei \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Osaka Gas\f28 \uc2 \f2  To Double Power Supply By FY20 -Nikkei \par Sojitz Targets Y100B Pretax Profit -Nikkei \par  \par  \par SCHEDULE OF MAIN EVENTS (times in GMT): \par 0200  Japan        Jul Electric Power Output \par 0300  New Zealand  Jul NZ Government bonds held for non-residents \par 0300  S Korea      2Q  Net barter terms of trade \par 0430  Japan        Jun Revised Industrial Production \par 0430  Japan        Jun Revised Retail Sales \par 0530  India        Jun IIP Index of Industrial Production \par 0530  France       2Q  GDP - preliminary figures \par 0530  France       Jul CPI \par 0645  France       2Q  Flash estimate of job creation \par 0830  Hong Kong    2Q  GDP \par 0900  EU           Jun Industrial Production \par 1130  India        Weekly \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves \par 1230  US           Jul Advance Sales for Retail & Food Services \par 1355  US           August Thomson Reuters / University of Michigan Survey of \par                    Consumers - preliminary \par 1400  US           Jun Manufacturing & Trade: Inventories & Sales \par 1400  US           New York Fed Pres Dudley participates in briefing on local \par                    economy in New York \par N/A   China        Jul Broad Money M2 \par  \par -By Helen Ozolins, Dow Jones Newswires; +64 4 471 5990; helen.ozolins@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 11-08-11 2211GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1160 4119   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110811e78b001nn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
