\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 877 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 August 2011\par 06:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par UPDATE: AUSTRALIAN JOBLESS RATE RISE ADD TO RATE CUT CHATTER \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australia's unemployment rate rose for the first time since mid-2010 in July, confirming that conditions in parts of the economy not exposed to the mining boom have slowedb down. The fall could raise chances of a possible cut in interest rates by the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Reserve Bank of Australia\f28 \uc2 \f2 , or RBA, especially if global markets remain on edge. (published 0248 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: BANK OF KOREA HOLDS POLICY STEADY, CITING CONCERNS ABOUT GLOBAL ECONOMY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bank of Korea kept its benchmark interest rate on hold for a second straight month as it opts to tread cautiously in normalizing policy amid global market turmoil and increasing concerns about the health of Korea's major export markets. (published 0229 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par 2ND UPDATE: JAPAN MACHINERY ORDERS UP, BUT GLOBAL JITTERS CLOUD OUTLOOK \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese core machinery orders rose briskly in June as firms dialed up spending hit by the March 11 earthquake and tsunami, though global economic jitters are clouding the outlook for corporate investment as a strong yen prods more companies to move production overseas. (published 0315 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par JAPAN GOVT EDANO: MULLING ECON MEASURES IF GLOBAL MALAISE CONTINUES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Japanese government's top spokesman said Thursday that the government may take domestic economic measures to help those affected by the volatile \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market. (published 0222 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par JAPAN FIN MIN NODA: YEN MOVES CONTINUE TO BE ONE-SIDED \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "I think one-sided movements are continuing. I'll continue to keenly watch the market today," Yoshihiko Noda told reporters at the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . (published 2317 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par JAPAN GOVT OFFICIAL: YEN'S STRENGTH IS EXCESSIVE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A Japanese government official declined to comment on whether \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market Thursday but said that recent yen's rise has gone too far. (published 0052 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par BOK: DOWNSIDE RISKS TO KOREA GROWTH TO INCREASE ON GLOBAL WOES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bank of Korea said Thursday it sees increased risks of a slowdown in the domestic economy due mainly to external uncertainties, while an uptrend in consumer price inflation will likely persist for some time. (published 0214 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: S KOREA DOESN'T SEE NEED FOR MACROECONOMIC POLICY SHIFT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korea's government isn't planning any sudden shifts in its macroeconomic policy at this time and will continue monitoring market movements, a senior official at the Ministry of Strategy and Finance reiterated Thursday. (published 0124 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par AUSTRALIA AUGUST INFLATION EXPECTATIONS 2.7% VS 3.4% IN JULY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A survey of inflationary expectations shows Australian consumers expect prices to rise 2.7% over the next 12 months, within the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's 2%-3% target band, down from 3.4% in July. (published 0100 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par AUSTRALIA ECONOMY IS WELL PLACED - IMF \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australia's economy is well placed given a strong outlook for commodities prices but policy makers need to be wary of capacity and price pressures, the International Monetary Fund said in its annual Article IV statement. (published 2243 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: CURRENT EVENTS GIVE OPPORTUNITY TO BOOST YUAN FLEXIBILITY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China should use current turmoil in international markets as an opportunity to boost the flexibility of its exchange rate, a Chinese government scholar said in an essay distributed Thursday. (published 0233 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par PBOC PROFESSOR: NOT A GOOD TIME TO RAISE INTEREST RATES -PAPER \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's authorities should tolerate high inflation for a while and try not to increase interest rates given the complicated global and domestic economic environment, an academic with China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  wrote in a commentary piece on Thursday. (published 2359 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par FOREIGNERS CUT INDONESIA GOVERNMENT BOND HOLDINGS BY IDR1.43 TLN TO IDR247.90 TLN TUESDAY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Foreigners cut their holdings of rupiah-denominated government bonds Tuesday by IDR1.43 trillion from Monday to IDR247.90 trillion, data from the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  showed Thursday. (published 0047 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par HK: EXPECT FURTHER MARKET VOLATILITY THURSDAY AMID WEAK INVESTOR SENTIMENT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Hong Kong Financial Secretary John Tsang and the city's de-facto \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said they expect further market volatility Thursday following sharp overnight declines in markets in the U.S. and Europe. (published 0052 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par NEW ZEALAND MANUFACTURING ACTIVITY INDEX EASES SLIGHTLY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 New Zealand's manufacturing activity slowed again in July, but continued to remain in expansion mode, data released Thursday showed. (published 2316 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: THAI COMMERCE MIN SIGNALS WILL TOLERATE SOME BAHT RISE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Thailand's new Deputy Prime Minister and Commerce Minister Kittiratt Na Ranong has signalled the government's willingness to tolerate a certain extent of local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  appreciation, saying the baht's strength against the dollar will help lower inflationary pressures of imported goods. (published 0335 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par PHILIPPINE FINANCE CHIEF: WORLD SHOULD CONSIDER ALTERNATIVE RESERVE \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCY\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  TO US DOLLAR \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Philippine Finance Secretary Cesar Purisima on Thursday suggested that governments should consider alternatives to the dollar as a reserve \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the face of economic and fiscal concerns in the U.S. (published 0159 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par      ======= DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ======= \par  \par  \par WSJ: FED PLEDGE DOES LITTLE TO KEEP EUROPEAN BEARS AWAY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.S. Federal Reserve's pledge to maintain low interest rates came at a time when all three of Europe's main national stock indexes had fallen into bear-market territory. (published 2050 GMT) [ 11-08-11 0400GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110811e78b000ar\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
