\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 705 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 August 2011\par 04:06\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DATA SNAP: AUSTRALIAN JULY UNEMPLOYMENT RATE 5.1% \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australia's unemployment rate rose to a higher-than-expected seasonally adjusted 5.1% in July from 4.9% in June. The number of employed fell by 100, the Australian Bureau of Statistics said Thursday. (published 0133 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par JAPAN JUN CORE MACHINERY ORDERS +7.7% ON MO; EXPECTED +1.7% \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese core machinery orders rose 7.7% on month in June on upbeat demand in both the manufacturing and non-manufacturing sectors as firms continued to recover from the March 11 earthquake and tsunami, the Cabinet Office said Thursday. (published 2357 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par BOJ MULLING ADDITIONAL EASING MEASURES TO SUPPORT MARKET - REPORT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bank of Japan is considering additional easing measures to support the market in light of the large fall in stocks worldwide and the yen's persistent strength, the Asahi Shimbun reported in its Thursday morning edition. (published 2334 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par JAPAN FIN MIN NODA: YEN MOVES CONTINUE TO BE ONE-SIDED \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "I think one-sided movements are continuing. I'll continue to keenly watch the market today," Yoshihiko Noda told reporters at the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . (published 2317 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par JAPAN GOVT OFFICIAL: YEN'S STRENGTH IS EXCESSIVE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A Japanese government official declined to comment on whether \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market Thursday but said that recent yen's rise has gone too far. (published 0052 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: S KOREA DOESN'T SEE NEED FOR MACROECONOMIC POLICY SHIFT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korea's government isn't planning any sudden shifts in its macroeconomic policy at this time and will continue monitoring market movements, a senior official at the Ministry of Strategy and Finance reiterated Thursday. (published 0124 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par AUSTRALIA AUGUST INFLATION EXPECTATIONS 2.7% VS 3.4% IN JULY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A survey of inflationary expectations shows Australian consumers expect prices to rise 2.7% over the next 12 months, within the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's 2%-3% target band, down from 3.4% in July. (published 0100 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par AUSTRALIA ECONOMY IS WELL PLACED - IMF \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australia's economy is well placed given a strong outlook for commodities prices but policy makers need to be wary of capacity and price pressures, the International Monetary Fund said in its annual Article IV statement. (published 2243 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par PBOC PROFESSOR: NOT A GOOD TIME TO RAISE INTEREST RATES -PAPER \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's authorities should tolerate high inflation for a while and try not to increase interest rates given the complicated global and domestic economic environment, an academic with China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  wrote in a commentary piece on Thursday. (published 2359 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par FOREIGNERS CUT INDONESIA GOVERNMENT BOND HOLDINGS BY IDR1.43 TLN TO IDR247.90 TLN TUESDAY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Foreigners cut their holdings of rupiah-denominated government bonds Tuesday by IDR1.43 trillion from Monday to IDR247.90 trillion, data from the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  showed Thursday. (published 0047 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par HK: EXPECT FURTHER MARKET VOLATILITY THURSDAY AMID WEAK INVESTOR SENTIMENT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Hong Kong Financial Secretary John Tsang and the city's de-facto \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said they expect further market volatility Thursday following sharp overnight declines in markets in the U.S. and Europe. (published 0052 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par NEW ZEALAND MANUFACTURING ACTIVITY INDEX EASES SLIGHTLY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 New Zealand's manufacturing activity slowed again in July, but continued to remain in expansion mode, data released Thursday showed. (published 2316 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: RECORD-LOW YIELDS SPUR SOME INTO SALE OF THE CENTURY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 With benchmark interest rates at record lows and investors eager for higher returns, the University of Southern California and Mexico each sought buyers Wednesday for bonds that will mature in 100 years. (published 2055 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par PHILIPPINE FINANCE CHIEF: WORLD SHOULD CONSIDER ALTERNATIVE RESERVE \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCY\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  TO US DOLLAR \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Philippine Finance Secretary Cesar Purisima on Thursday suggested that governments should consider alternatives to the dollar as a reserve \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the face of economic and fiscal concerns in the U.S. (published 0159 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par      ======= DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ======= \par  \par  \par WSJ: FED PLEDGE DOES LITTLE TO KEEP EUROPEAN BEARS AWAY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.S. Federal Reserve's pledge to maintain low interest rates came at a time when all three of Europe's main national stock index