\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 927 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 August 2011\par 03:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par JAPAN JUN CORE MACHINERY ORDERS +7.7% ON MO; EXPECTED +1.7% \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese core machinery orders rose 7.7% on month in June on upbeat demand in both the manufacturing and non-manufacturing sectors as firms continued to recover from the March 11 earthquake and tsunami, the Cabinet Office said Thursday. (published 2357 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par PBOC PROFESSOR: NOT A GOOD TIME TO RAISE INTEREST RATES -PAPER \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's authorities should tolerate high inflation for a while and try not to increase interest rates given the complicated global and domestic economic environment, an academic with China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  wrote in a commentary piece on Thursday. (published 2359 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par BOJ MULLING ADDITIONAL EASING MEASURES TO SUPPORT MARKET - REPORT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bank of Japan is considering additional easing measures to support the market in light of the large fall in stocks worldwide and the yen's persistent strength, the Asahi Shimbun reported in its Thursday morning edition. (published 2334 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par JAPAN FIN MIN NODA: YEN MOVES CONTINUE TO BE ONE-SIDED \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "I think one-sided movements are continuing. I'll continue to keenly watch the market today," Yoshihiko Noda told reporters at the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . (published 2317 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par JAPAN GOVT OFFICIAL: YEN'S STRENGTH IS EXCESSIVE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A Japanese government official declined to comment on whether \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market Thursday but said that recent yen's rise has gone too far. (published 0052 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par AUSTRALIA AUGUST INFLATION EXPECTATIONS 2.7% VS 3.4% IN JULY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A survey of inflationary expectations shows Australian consumers expect prices to rise 2.7% over the next 12 months, within the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's 2%-3% target band, down from 3.4% in July. (published 0100 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par S KOREA OFFICIAL: DON'T SEE NEED FOR MACROECONOMIC POLICY SHIFT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korea's government isn't planning any sudden shifts in its macroeconomic policy at this time, a senior official at the Ministry of Strategy and Finance said Thursday. (published 0045 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par AUSTRALIA ECONOMY IS WELL PLACED - IMF \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australia's economy is well placed given a strong outlook for commodities prices but policy makers need to be wary of capacity and price pressures, the International Monetary Fund said in its annual Article IV statement. (published 2243 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par FOREIGNERS CUT INDONESIA GOVERNMENT BOND HOLDINGS BY IDR1.43 TLN TO IDR247.90 TLN TUESDAY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Foreigners cut their holdings of rupiah-denominated government bonds Tuesday by IDR1.43 trillion from Monday to IDR247.90 trillion, data from the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  showed Thursday. (published 0047 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par HK: EXPECT FURTHER MARKET VOLATILITY THURSDAY AMID WEAK INVESTOR SENTIMENT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Hong Kong Financial Secretary John Tsang and the city's de-facto \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said they expect further market volatility Thursday following sharp overnight declines in markets in the U.S. and Europe. (published 0052 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par NEW ZEALAND MANUFACTURING ACTIVITY INDEX EASES SLIGHTLY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 New Zealand's manufacturing activity slowed again in July, but continued to remain in expansion mode, data released Thursday showed. (published 2316 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par NZ JULY MEDIAN HOUSE PRICE NZ$345,000 VS NZ$360,000 IN JUNE - REINZ \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 New Zealand home prices fell in July from June, the Real Estate Institute of New Zealand said Thursday. (published 2255 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CHINA HALTS PROPERTY FIRMS' GUARANTEE OF OFFSHORE UNITS' BOND SALES -REPORT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  regulator has stopped accepting applications from property companies to review their applications to offer guarantees on bonds sold by offshore units, the state-run Shanghai Securities News reported Thursday. (published 2329 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par BULLS UNWIND BETS IN EM, BUT INSTABILITY LESS A RISK THIS TIME \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Traders and investors are seeing the beginnings of an unwind in popular bullish trades in emerging-market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . (published 2202 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par REID: PATENT AND TRADE BILLS WILL TOP SENATE AGENDA \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Senate Majority Leader Harry Reid, said Wednesday that patent and trade legislation will top the agenda when Senators return to Washington next month following the August recess. (published 1953 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: S&P: FORCING RATING FIRMS TO DISCLOSE ERRORS COMPROMISES FIRMS' INDEPENDENCE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Standard & Poor's\f28 \uc2 \f2  Ratings Services is taking issue with what mistakes should have to be revealed to regulators, after it downgraded the U.S. government's credit rating Friday and, in response, U.S. officials said S&P made a $2 trillion error. (published 2148 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: RECORD-LOW YIELDS SPUR SOME INTO SALE OF THE CENTURY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 With benchmark interest rates at record lows and investors eager for higher returns, the University of Southern California and Mexico each sought buyers Wednesday for bonds that will mature in 100 years. (published 2055 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par WSJ: FISHER AND KOCHERLAKOTA PRAISE BERNANKE DESPITE DISSENTS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Richard Fisher and Narayana Kocherlakota, two of the three Federal Reserve officials who dissented from the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's latest decision to keep interest rates near zero for two years, offered strong praise for Fed Chairman Ben Bernanke a day after voting against his push toward easier monetary policy. (published 2039 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CANADA FIN MIN: EUROZONE POLITICS COULD SLOW DOWN POLICY MEASURES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Canada's Finance Minister, Jim Flaherty, warned Wednesday that Europe's political system, where there is no single finance ministry, could slow down the introduction of policies needed to stabilize financial markets. (published 2053 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par     ======= DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ======= \par  \par  \par WSJ: FED PLEDGE DOES LITTLE TO KEEP EUROPEAN BEARS AWAY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.S. Federal Reserve's pledge to maintain low interest rates came at a time when all three of Europe's main national stock indexes had fallen into bear-market territory. (published 2050 GMT) [ 11-08-11 0100GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110811e78b0002f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw302\pic