\par\par \b \uc2 Market update: Michael Wood-Martin, manager of the Henderson Horizon Japanese Equity fund\b0\par\par\uc2 922 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 August 2011\par \uc2 M2 Presswire\par \uc2 MTPW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 2011, M2 Communications. All rights reserved.   \par \par \uc2 Markets around the world have been weak over the past several weeks as investors are beginning to discount a somewhat slower economic growth environment in the year ahead. So where does this leave the Japanese economy and stock market?\par \par \uc2 The Japanese economy is beginning to recover stronger than we had expected as companies begin to bring back production schedules post the earthquake back in March of this year. And we think this is a trend that\u8217\'20\'19s going to continue over the next several months, so the corporate sector is in pretty good shape.\par \par \uc2 An interesting part of the economy we\u8217\'20\'19ve noticed recently has been the consumer which has been stronger than we had expected as consumption levels have picked up. This may be just a recovery after the lull post the earthquake, but ultimately, it may signify something a bit more meaningful within the Japanese economy where consumers, having been rather reticent to spend over the past couple of years, or even the past couple of decades, are beginning to really find their feet in terms of consumption and just spend a bit more money on themselves. And we\u8217\'20\'19ve seen this from various company management teams that have come through the office here in London, such as housing-related companies who have mentioned that they\u8217\'20\'19ve seen a big pickup in orders, not just in the earthquake struck area, but around the country. And it may be that consumers, having built up a lot of cash over the past number of years are deciding to spend a bit more. So this is an area to which we\u8217\'20\'19re paying a lot more attention to within the portfolio.\par \par \uc2 So, for the economy as a whole, the corporate sector is getting back on track. The consumer is better than we had expected; and I think that the overall rebuilding demand within Japan should propel economic growth over the next 12 months.\par \par \uc2 Another aspect which is worth mentioning is the yen, which has been very strong against the dollar. The dollar is a very important \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for Japanese exporters, and we have seen some action by the authorities, both the Bank of Japan and the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , to take action against the strong yen to support the export sector.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of Japan has been quite active in, shall we say, increasing the purchases of private sector assets, and again, this is something which will probably continue.\par \par \uc2 So, the authorities are taking action to alleviate any pressure that might be coming upon the economy, and also on the stock market.\par \par \uc2 So where does this leave the stock market? We are now back down to levels that we saw in 2003 and 2009, from which the market rallied. At this level I feel that the market is pretty much bullet proof. The price to book in the market in terms of asset value is less than one times, so again, pretty much limited on the downside there. We are seeing corporates increasing dividends and share buybacks to shareholders; so again, the dividend yield is now at least the same as, if not higher than the S&P 500. And ultimately, there isn\u8217\'20\'19t the excess to squeeze from the Japanese economy that might be the case in other economies. So again, I feel that the Japanese economy and the Japanese stock market at these levels is pretty much at rock bottom levels.\par \par \uc2 I mentioned the yen has been very strong; and Japanese government bond yields, have fallen. However, it looks like perhaps these two attributes (rising Yen and falling bond yields) are coming towards a peak.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  \u8216\'20\'18s \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  has recently \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the F/X market to stem the yen\u8217\'20\'19s rise and they may do so again to alleviate unnecessary pressure on the economy from a rapidly strengthening \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Alongside this the Bank of Japan actions in buying more private sector assets could assist a recovery in asset prices. In the past where we have seen a slackening off in the yen and a rise in bond yields, it has proved very good for the Japanese equity market.\par \par \uc2 So again, pretty much rock bottom in terms of equity market levels, and we should be at a level now where the equity market can make some gains.\par \par \uc2 In terms of the portfolio, I mentioned that I\u8217\'20\'19m fairly big in consumption within Japan and so I have some big holdings in stocks such as Rakuten, the online retailer; and Yamada Denki, which sells electrical goods. Both companies have had very good quarter one results, and the outlook for the remainder of the year is upbeat. So, I think the consumer area within Japan is a good place to be.\par \par \uc2 I\u8217\'20\'19m overweight the domestic areas within my portfolio. I\u8217\'20\'19m slightly shy of the overseas areas,., largely because it\u8217\'20\'19s a very competitive marketplace; and I think it\u8217\'20\'19s going to become even more so if, demand in developed markets begins to slacken off.\par \par \uc2 So the portfolio has a domestic bias focusing on the consumer and away from, shall we say, more the traditional companies associated with Japan; and light in the auto sector.\par \par \uc2 M2 Communications disclaims all liability for information provided within M2 PressWIRE. Data supplied by named party/parties. Further information on M2 PressWIRE can be obtained at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.presswire.net"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 