\par\par \b \uc2 WORLD  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 :Euro Strengthens With Recovery In Risk Sentiment\b0\par\par\uc2 By Anusha Shrivastava   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 503 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 August 2011\par 23:03\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global FX & Fixed Income News\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--The euro strengthened Thursday as negative euro zone headlines were unable to dent risk sentiment and the rally in the U.S. equities markets provided a boost.\par \par \uc2 Early in the day, the Swiss franc plunged against the euro and the dollar with rampant speculation that Switzerland would resort to dramatic measures to weaken its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Some even suggested Switzerland could take the drastic step of pegging its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the euro, which itself has been hurt by Europe's sovereign debt crisis.\par \par \uc2 "By pegging the franc to the euro, Switzerland would repeat the mistake that China has made by pegging the Yuan to the dollar," said Peter Schiff, chief executive officer of brokerage firm Euro Pacific Capital. "Such a move would add to global imbalances, deny investors an important safe haven \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and place an unfair tax on the Swiss people who will no longer enjoy the benefits of a strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 As the day progressed, the rally in the U.S. stock markets took center stage as the Dow gained nearly 500 points.\par \par \uc2 For Friday, market participants are pegging hopes on stronger data to keep the mood buoyant. July's retail sales figures and the University of Michigan's consumer sentiment data will be posted, providing some indicators for the U.S. economy.\par \par \uc2 "It is difficult to gauge swings in the risk complex at this time and on Friday, we could extend this move if the data are positive," said Dan Katzive, director in the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy research team at \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  in New York. "The market has not shown a lot of persistence. Market sentiment is very sensitive to headline risk and data so it could be hurt by adverse headlines."\par \par \uc2 Late Thursday, the euro was at $1.4241 from $1.4178 late Wednesday, according to EBS via CQG. The dollar was at Y76.83 from Y76.86, while the euro was at Y109.29 from Y108.966. The U.K. pound was at $1.6230 from $1.6130. The dollar was at CHF0.7620 from CHF0.72660.\par \par \uc2 The ICE Dollar Index, which tracks the U.S. dollar against a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , was at about 74.636, unchanged on the day.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets are also waiting to see if the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  and some expect a fresh round of yen selling by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  soon.\par \par \uc2 The BOJ last week spent around $52 billion buying dollars against the yen in an effort to cap the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's appreciation, but the move failed to have a lasting impact as global economic growth concerns and heightened fiscal worries in the U.S. and Europe spurred investors to seek shelter in safe-haven \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  like the yen.\par \par \uc2 -By Anusha Shrivastava, Dow Jones Newswires; 212-416-2227; anusha.shrivastava@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 - Javier E. David contributed to this report [ 08-11-11 1703ET ]\par \par \uc2 1033 1074 1158 1863 1865 20714 28038 4164   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110811e78b00056\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
