\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Fed's zero rate commitment is close to BOJ's -Shirakawa\b0\par\par\uc2 336 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 August 2011\par 07:19\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * BOJ ready to take policy action if needed -Shirakawa\par \par \uc2 * BOJ determined to avoid repeat of last summer\par \par \uc2 (Adds quote, detail)\par \par \uc2 By Leika Kihara\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Aug 10 (Reuters) - Bank of Japan Governor Masaaki Shirakawa signalled that his bank was ahead of its U.S. counterpart in easing monetary policy aggressively, not the other way around as some critics say, stressing that the Federal Reserve's commitment on near-zero rates is similiar to what the BOJ already has in place.\par \par \uc2 Shirakawa said the BOJ acted pre-emptively with last week's monetary easing by taking into account various uncertainties on the country's economic outlook, such as the potential damage from recent yen rises and slowing U.S. growth.\par \par \uc2 While signalling that the BOJ was ready to ease policy further if economic conditions worsen, Shirakawa countered the view held by some lawmakers and market players that it was less willing and slower to loosen credit than the Fed.\par \par \uc2 "Under our comprehensive monetary easing steps adopted last October, we pledged to keep interest rates effectively at zero until price stability can be foreseen," Shirakawa told parliament on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 "The Fed's latest commitment is close to what we already have in place," Shirakawa said, referring to the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's pledge on Wednesday to keep interest rates near zero until mid-2013.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and eased monetary policy last week in an effort to keep sharp yen rises from derailing a fragile economy.\par \par \uc2 The BOJ was criticised last summer for responding too slowly to yen rises against the dollar, driven by market views that the Fed would ease policy more aggressively than the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 BOJ officials hope to avoid a repeat of this and have expressed the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's determination to ease policy pre-emptively to counter any risks to the Japanese economy.\par \par \uc2 (Editing by Joseph Radford)\par \par \uc2 JAPAN-ECONOMY/SHIRAKAWA-FED (UPDATE 1)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110813e78a00035\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
