\par\par \b \uc2 WRAPUP 1-Japan says watching markets with urgency as stocks plunge\b0\par\par\uc2 643 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 August 2011\par 03:55\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Japan finance minister: watching markets with urgency after stocks rout\par \par \uc2 * Yen heading towards levels prior to last week's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 * BOJ chief highlights increased overseas risks to Japanese economy\par \par \uc2 (.)\par \par \uc2 By Tetsushi Kajimoto and Leika Kihara\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Aug 9 (Reuters) - Japanese policymakers voiced growing alarm on Tuesday over upheavals in financial markets and a worsening global economic outlook as stocks plunged and the yen edged back to highs scaled prior to last week's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda said he would watch markets with a sense of urgency after stocks tumbled on recession fears, while Bank of Japan Governor Masaaki Shirakawa said the uncertain outlook for the world economy posed a major risk for Japan.\par \par \uc2 Tokyo's Nikkei share average plunged more than 4 percent, falling below 9,000 for the first time since mid-March, after Wall Street lost more than 6 percent in a flight-to-safety sell-off following the U.S. government's loss of its top credit rating.\par \par \uc2 "I will pay close attention to market movements with a sense of urgency today," Noda told reporters when asked about the stock market falls and a persistently strong yen despite \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's solo \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  last week to stem its rise.\par \par \uc2 The Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was trading at around 77.35 yen against the dollar, close to a four-month high of 76.29 yen hit last week before Tokyo \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  and way off post-\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels of more than 80 yen to the dollar.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold a record of more than 4 trillion yen last Thursday to prevent the yen's climb from derailing the economy's recovery from the damage wrought by the triple-blow of a massive earthquake and tsunami and a radiation crisis at a damaged nuclear power plant.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also stepped in, loosening monetary policy by boosting its asset buying scheme by half to 15 trillion yen in a move aimed both at making the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  more effective and shoring up market confidence.\par \par \uc2 But the effects of the joint effort, Japan's third foray into \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets in less than a year, have worn off quickly as fears that twin debt crises in the United States and Europe could tip the world economy back into recession drove investors into low-risk assets such as the yen, gold and Swiss franc.\par \par \uc2 Shirakawa told parliament he still expected the world's third largest economy to return to moderate growth, but highlighted increased overseas risks to such a scenario.\par \par \uc2 Minutes of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's meeting in July showed that its board was increasingly worried about the global economic outlook and two of its members were already advocating further monetary easing.\par \par \uc2 Despite Japan's own troubles with public debt twice the size of the $5 trillion economy, post-quake recession and political stalemate, its deep bond market is seen as one of the relatively few safe investments both by Japanese and foreign investors.\par \par \uc2 Global stock markets plunged on Monday as the G7 finance ministers' and central bankers pledge over the weekend to help smooth markets if needed provided little reassurance.\par \par \uc2 The European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  swept into the bond market to buy up Italian and Spanish debt and sling a safety net under the euro zone's third- and fourth-largest economies. But bickering persisted in Europe over a longer-term rescue plan.\par \par \uc2 Noda said Monday's G7 statement helped to ease market uneasiness and he would keep in close contact with his G7 partners in the coming weeks.\par \par \uc2 The finance minister declined to comment on whether he will run in the ruling party leadership race, saying he will fulfil his duty as a member of Prime Minister Naoto Kan's cabinet given the current circumstances.\par \par \uc2 (Writing by Tomasz Janowski)\par \par \uc2 JAPAN-ECONOMY/NODA  (WRAPUP 1)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110812e7890002v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
