\par\par \b \uc2 ASIAN MORNING BRIEFING: US Stocks Plunge In Sixth Biggest DJIA Decline In History\b0\par\par\uc2 2,984 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 August 2011\par 00:11\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 Singapore markets are closed Tuesday for National Day.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DJIA      10809.85 -634.76  -5.55% \par Nasdaq     2357.69 -174.72  -6.90% \par S&P 500    1119.46  -79.92  -6.66% \par FTSE 100   5068.95 -178.04  -3.39% \par Xetra DAX  5923.27 -312.89  -5.02% \par  \par 2Y Tsy  100 7/32   +2/32 0.264%  -2.8BP \par 5Y Tsy 101 31/32  +26/32 1.084% -16.9BP \par 10Y Tsy 107 1/32 +2 4/32 2.318% -23.8BP \par  \par USD/JPY    77.65-68  -0.41% \par Range      78.46-77.50 \par EUR/USD    1.4173-76  -1.67% \par Range      1.4430-1.4130 \par AUD/USD    1.0194-98  -2.59% \par Range      1.0445-1.0184 \par  \par USD/JPY 1M Vol    11.70-12.20% \par EUR/USD 1M Vol    14.50-15.02% \par AUD/USD 1M Vol    15.50-16.25% \par  \par Comex Gold         $1,713.20 +$61.40 \par Nymex Crude (NY)      $81.31  -$5.57 \par  \par  \par U.S. SUMMARY & ASIAN OUTLOOK: \par STOCKS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 U.S. stocks tumbled in a Monday rout that sent the Dow Jones Industrial Average plunging to the sixth biggest point drop in its history, reflecting a toxic brew of investor fears over government debt and the chances that the economy will slide into another recession.\par \par \uc2 The Dow Jones Industrial Average sank 634.76 points, or 5.55%, to 10809.85, falling beneath 11000 for the first time since November and adding to last week's steep losses. The blue-chip measure ended exactly on session lows in the biggest single-day point and percentage loss since Dec. 1, 2008.\par \par \uc2 Monday was the stock market's first trading day since \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  downgraded the federal government's credit rating late Friday. Investors fled risk assets far and wide. They traded 9.71 billion shares in New York Stock Exchange composite volume, the fourth largest single-day total in history.\par \par \uc2 "Everybody is looking for whatever they perceive as a safe haven, even if it's just plain illogical," said David Kelly, chief market strategist for \uc2 J.P. Morgan\uc2  Funds. "Things are pretty dismal right now."\par \par \uc2 Major stock indexes cascaded lower throughout much of the session, as S&P downgraded clearing bodies, entities such as \uc2 Fannie Mae\uc2  and \uc2 Freddie Mac\uc2  and lowered outlooks for companies including \uc2 Warren Buffett\uc2 's \uc2 Berkshire Hathaway\uc2  following S&P's downgrade of U.S. credit to double-A-plus from triple-A late Friday.\par \par \uc2 The Standard & Poor's 500 stock index tumbled 79.92 points, or 6.66%, to 1119.46, the 10th decline in 11 sessions. Financial components fell 10% while S&P 500 energy stocks slumped 8.3%. Not a single component of the 500 stocks that make up the broad index finished in positive territory. The Nasdaq Composite slumped 174.72 points, or 6.9%, to 2357.69.\par \par \uc2 The Russell 2000 index of small-capitalization stocks fell the most in a single session in its history, plummeting 63.67 points, or 8.91%, to 650.96. Small caps tend to make more exaggerated moves and are generally viewed as riskier than the widely held shares of large companies.\par \par \uc2 The fact that Monday's swoon came right on the heels of the Dow's biggest weekly point loss since the financial crisis in 2008 set up many market participants for forced sales and margin calls, traders said. That made some of the losses self-perpetuating. It also raised the prospect of capitulation, the point when losses snowball and sentiment craters, helping markets find a bottom.\par \par \uc2 "There is a lot of forced liquidation," said Lorenzo Di Mattia, manager of Sibilla Global Fund, and such actions "might last another day perhaps."\par \par \uc2 In one early sign that Monday's action would be volatile, the New York Stock Exchange invoked the little-used Rule 48 before the start of trading. The procedure lets market makers refrain from disseminating price indications ahead of the bell, making it easier and faster to open trading in the stock market.\par \par \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  plunged $1.66, or 20%, to 6.51, to lead blue-chip decliners, stung by both a steep selloff in financial stocks and by word that \uc2 American International Group\uc2  is suing the company, along with a host of other prominent financial institutions, as it seeks to recover losses on mortgage-backed securities. AIG's stock fell 2.52, or 10%, to 22.58.\par \par \uc2 Gold miners and precious-metals exchange-traded funds bucked the broader trend as they followed the precious metal higher. \uc2 AngloGold Ashanti\uc2  rose 20 cents, or 0.5%, to 42.01 and \uc2 SPDR Gold Trust\uc2  gained 5.37, or 3.3%, to 167.12. \uc2 Newmont Mining\uc2  gained early but ended down 28 cents, or 0.5%, at 54.13.\par \par \uc2 China's shares fell sharply to a more than one-year low Monday, as \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  decision Friday to lower the U.S.'s credit rating raised fresh concerns about a global economic slump that could lead to a hard landing in China.\par \par \uc2 In a move apparently aimed at soothing the market, the state-run Xinhua News Agency issued a commentary a few minutes before the market closed calling on investors to be calm as the U.S. still has one of the world's most competitive economies. However, analysts said sentiment surrounding China shares will remain bearish until Beijing rolls out pro-market measures to support stock prices.\par \par \uc2 "If there is a second round of the global financial crisis brought about by renewed economic downturns in Europe and the U.S., China may not escape a hard landing this time," said Zhou Xu, an analyst with Nanjing Securities Co.\par \par \uc2 Hong Kong shares also extended losses to end lower Monday.\par \par \uc2 Analysts said they expect the domestic stock market to remain volatile over the next few sessions and the city's benchmark index to trade between 19,800 and 20,800 points this week, amid concerns about slower economic growth after the U.S. downgrade.\par \par \uc2 "The panic sell off...has factored in S&P's downgrade of the U.S. credit rating, and the benchmark index may rebound a bit later this week as stocks are oversold, with an undemanding valuation of around 10 times projected 2011 earnings," said Kenny Tang, general manager of AMTD Financial Planning.\par \par \uc2 He said investors will be on the lookout for more trading cues from U.S. Federal Reserve Chairman Ben Bernanke after Tuesday's policy meeting.\par \par \uc2 Tanrich investment manager Jackson Wong noted investors will also focus on China's July consumer price index data scheduled for Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 He added there are concerns that other ratings agencies, such as Fitch and \uc2 Moody's\uc2 , could also downgrade the U.S. government's credit rating.\par \par \uc2 Tokyo stocks fell Monday as \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  decision late Friday to strip the U.S. of its triple-A credit rating sent a shockwave through the market, driving the Nikkei Stock Average to close at its lowest level since March 17.\par \par \uc2 Oil futures fell to their lowest level in almost nine months in U.S. trading Monday, as the \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  downgrade of U.S. debt highlighted the weak economic health of the world's biggest oil consumer.\par \par \uc2 Gold soared to a record in U.S. trading Monday as investors scrambled to shed riskier holdings in the wake of a U.S. credit downgrade. The December contract settled at a record $1,713.20 a troy ounce on the Comex division of the \uc2 New York Mercantile Exchange\uc2 , and set an intraday record of $1,723.40 a troy ounce after floor trading closed.\par \par \uc2 Base metal prices took a battering Monday, closing sharply lower across the board after \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  downgrade of U.S. debt drove investors out of risky investments and into safe haven assets such as gold.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 It was the euro, not the dollar, that bore the brunt of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Standard & Poor's\f28 \uc2 \f2  downgrade of the U.S. credit rating to double-A-plus Monday. Rattled traders ran to the Swiss franc and the yen, abandoning riskier \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  more closely tied to economic growth.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro was the focal point of a massive move away from nearly all higher-yielding \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , at one point trading down as much as 3% against the safe-harbor Swiss franc, hitting a new all-time low of CHF1.0619. The dollar and U.K. pound also hit new all-time lows against the franc, though their declines were milder.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Many analysts believe the euro is still stronger than it should be, given that most of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's gains came on projections of strong global economic growth. With the U.S. potentially slipping back into recession, and even emerging markets showing some signs of slower expansion, the euro could stand to drop to its true value estimated of $1.20, rather than Monday's $1.42.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro zone's debt problems also appear to be worsening, further undermining the common \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . On Monday, French credit-default swaps widened significantly on fears that France also faces a downgrade from its triple-A rating. Traders view such a development as possibly the next serious stress point for the euro zone because France's triple-A rating helps back a triple-A rated European Financial Stability Facility. That fund is viewed as an essential emergency bailout fund for the euro zone's most vulnerable economies should they run into fresh troubles.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The French AAA underpins the AAA rating of the [European Financial Stability Facility] and any downgrade would put the entire rescue mechanism in question," said Steven Englander, head of G10 strategy at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Citigroup\f28 \uc2 \f2  in New York.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 That French news overwhelmed news that the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  also bought Italian and Spanish bonds for the first time, driving yields on Italian and Spanish debt sharply lower.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Late Monday, the euro was at $1.4180 from $1.4275 late Friday, according to EBS via CQG. The dollar was at Y77.79 from Y78.42, while the euro was at Y110.27 from Y112.01. The U.K. pound was at $1.6320 from $1.6394. The dollar was at CHF0.7548 from CHF0.7675.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The ICE Dollar Index, which tracks the U.S. dollar against a basket of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , was at 74.852 from about 74.598.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 While it was U.S. debt that was downgraded Friday, the dollar retained enough appeal as a relatively stable "safe haven" to attract nervous investors Monday. Fears of an already fragile U.S. fiscal picture getting worse feeds into existing concern that the U.S. economy could drag down other healthier economies on the globe. With the U.S. debt-ceiling talks and any imminent debt default out of the way, the dollar could again resume its function as a safe-haven \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  along with the franc and the yen. [ 08-08-11 2211GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Traders are increasingly taking into account a U.S. recession," said Paresh Upadhyaya, head of Americas G10 \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategy at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of America\f28 \uc2 \f2 Merrill Lynch\f28 \uc2 \f2  in New York.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Other safe havens also face the threat of government \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Central banks in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and Switzerland have both recently attempted to halt their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ' rise, without much success so far.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Monday, investors ignored the threat of further Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by selling off the dollar by 1% against the yen. Commodity-linked \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  also weakened as the price of oil and stocks continued to fall throughout the day. Both the Australian and New Zealand dollars fell nearly 2% against the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par BONDS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Treasury bonds proved Monday that they're still the go-to safe-haven for many investors at time of stress, even though the U.S.'s rating was cut by Standard and Poor's for the first time in history last week.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Rather than sparking a selloff in Treasurys, S&P's action--cutting the U.S.'s triple-A rating one notch to double-A-plus--added to fears about the global economic outlook. The result was a broad-based selloff in risky assets, with the Dow Jones Industrial Average stock index plunging by 5.5% and a flight into safe-haven Treasurys and gold.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The ferocious buying sent Treasury yields, which move inversely to their prices, tumbling to fresh landmark lows. The two-year note's yield fell to a fresh record low of 0.228% while the benchmark 10-year note's yield dipped to as low as 2.309%, the weakest level since January 2009. The yield has nosedived from this year's peak of 3.77% in February and is now trading near the record low of 2.034% hit in mid December 2008 after \f28 \uc2 \f2 Lehman Brothers\f28 \uc2 \f2 's collapse.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It is not about yields," said Thomas Roth, executive director in the U.S. government bond trading group at Mitsubishi UFJ Securities (USA) Inc. in New York. "People are buying Treasurys because they are afraid to be in anything risky. They will take Treasurys at whatever yield they can get."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In late Monday afternoon trade in New York, the 10-year note was 2 4/32 higher in price to yield 2.318%. The 30-year bond was 3 6/32 higher to yield 3.657%, and the two-year note was 2/32 higher to yield 0.264%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The price move underlines the major dilemma confronting investors: There are few alternative safe-haven assets out there that can match the depth and liquidity of the Treasury market, which has more than $9.3 trillion in debt outstanding.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Double-A-plus is the de facto triple A," said James Paulsen, chief investment strategist at Minneapolis-based \f28 \uc2 \f2 Wells Capital Management\f28 \uc2 \f2 , which manages $342 billion. "There is a lot of fear in the markets right now and Treasurys are still the place to go."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese government bonds rose Monday as investors took a cue from weak share prices, although investors stayed cautious on how U.S. Treasurys would perform later in the global day.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The JGB market was also supported by the impending 40-year JGB auction Tuesday. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will sell Y400 billion of bonds with an expected coupon of 2.2%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 RBS Securities senior strategist Akito Fukunaga called the new issue "cheap in many ways." He said the 40-year yield--little-changed from the previous auction--was "attractive" compared with the 20-, 30-year yields, which have been declining.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's government bond prices ended higher Monday amid increased demand for safe-haven assets after the U.S. credit-rating downgrade triggered a heavy selloff in global equity markets.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 However, traders are still cautious about the outlook for bond prices in the medium- to long-term, as mounting inflation remains a major concern for China's policy makers.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China is scheduled to release July inflation data Tuesday. The median forecast of 13 economists surveyed by Dow Jones Newswires is for inflation to have remained high in July at 6.3%. China's consumer price index rose 6.4% in June from a year earlier, the highest growth in three years.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Among other Chinese data due Tuesday, July PPI likely rose 7.5% on the year, up from June's 7.1% increase, mainly as a result of a low base of comparison, according to the poll. Industrial production growth likely slowed to 14.8% on the year in July, down from 15.1% growth in June, while January-July non-rural FAI likely rose 25.5% on the year, easing from a rise of 25.6% on the year in the January-June period.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Among other events, Indonesia's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will likely keep its policy rate, the BI rate, unchanged at 6.75% for the sixth consecutive month when it holds its rate-setting meeting Tuesday, according to all 11 economists polled by Dow Jones.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par TOP HEADLINES: \par Obama Stresses Urgency On Tackling Deficit \par AIG Sues Bank Of America Over Mortgages \par ECB Buys Italian, Spanish Bonds \par MGM Resorts Swings To Profit On China IPO \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Capital One\f28 \uc2 \f2  In Talks For \f28 \uc2 \f2 HSBC\f28 \uc2 \f2  Card Unit \par Valeant Approached Medicis About A Sale \par Arab Nations Condemn Syria As Violence Mounts \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Moody's\f28 \uc2 \f2  Sees US Economic Recovery Continuing \par E*Trade Hires \f28 \uc2 \f2 Goldman Sachs\f28 \uc2 \f2  For Review \par S Korea July Producer Prices +6.5% On Year Vs +6.2% June \par Mitsubishi Heavy Pres: Not Eyeing Full Business Merger With Hitachi-Report \par  \par  \par SCHEDULE OF MAIN EVENTS (times in GMT): \par 2245  New Zealand  Jul Electronic Card Transactions \par 2350  Japan        Jul Bank of Japan Monetary Policy Meeting Minutes \par 2350  Japan        Jul Money Stock, Broadly-defined Liquidity \par 0130  Australia    Jun Housing Finance \par 0130  Australia    Jul NAB Business Survey \par 0200  China        Jul CPI \par 0200  China        Jul PPI \par 0200  China        Jul Industrial Output \par 0200  China        Jul Fixed Assets Investment \par 0200  China        Jul Retail sales \par 0500  Japan        Jul Consumer Confidence Survey \par 0600  Japan        Jul Preliminary Machine Tool Orders \par 0600  Germany      Jun Foreign Trade \par 0630  Indonesia    Aug Bank Indonesia Board of Governors meeting & decision \par 0700  Germany      Jun Deutsche Bundesbank - Current Account \par 0830  UK           Jun UK trade \par 0830  UK           Jun UK monthly industrial production figures \par 1130  US           Jul NFIB Index of Small Business Optimism \par 1145  US           Aug 6 ICSC-\f28 \uc2 \f2 Goldman Sachs\f28 \uc2 \f2  Chain Store Sales Index \par 1230  US           2Q  Preliminary Productivity & Costs \par 1255  US           Aug 6 Johnson Redbook Retail Sales Index \par 1400  US           Aug IBD/TIPP Economic Optimism Index \par 1400  UK           Jul NIESR Monthly GDP Estimates \par 1815  US           Federal Reserve Board - U.S. interest rate decision \par 2030  US           Aug 5 API Weekly Statistical Bulletin \par N/A   Indonesia    Jul International Reserves \par N/A   EU           European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  conducts longer-term refinancing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par N/A   US           State of Wisconsin recall elections as \par                    Democrats try and remove six Republicans from office \par  \par -By Helen Ozolins, Dow Jones Newswires; +64 4 471 59