\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 4-\uc2 Moody's\uc2  warns against Japan  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  action, BOJ on alert\b0\par\par\uc2 867 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 August 2011\par 01:48\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 * Ineffective \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  action negative for Japan credit rating - \uc2 Moody's\par \par \uc2 * G7 agreed excessive, disorderly \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves undesirable -Noda\par \par \uc2 * BOJ holds off on draining funds from \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 * BOJ on alert vs further yen rise, eyes on FOMC\par \par \uc2 (Adds \uc2 Moody's\uc2  quote, details)\par \par \uc2 By Stanley White and Leika Kihara\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Aug 8 (Reuters) - Moody's Investors Service warned \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  that ineffective \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be negative for its sovereign ratings and would not help it restore its finances, even as G7 policymakers tried to show solidarity against market turmoil sparked by U.S. and European debt woes.\par \par \uc2 The warning was a shot across the bow for Japan, saddled with public debt double the size of its $5 trillion economy, just days after the United States lost its top-tier AAA credit rating from \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda on Monday signalled Tokyo's readiness to continue \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to stem yen rises, citing a Group of Seven agreement to jointly counter any excessive and disorderly exchange-rate moves.\par \par \uc2 Moody's\uc2 , however, said that while \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's solo \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and monetary easing last week initially pushed the yen lower against the dollar, the effect proved short-lived and was a negative for the economy and its credit rating.\par \par \uc2 "Yen strength has eroded the competitiveness of Japan's exports and hampered the economy's ability to sustain its recovery from the 2009 global recession," \uc2 Moody's\uc2  said.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and monetary policy action alone will not solve the bigger problems plaguing Japan, such as the huge cost for reconstruction from the quake and the nuclear plant disaster, as well as much-needed social welfare reforms to restore the country's fiscal health, \uc2 Moody's\uc2  said in a statement on Monday.\par \par \uc2 Major ratings agencies have all put Japan's sovereign rating on negative outlook as the country struggles to balance the need to support its economy, hit by a deadly earthquake in March, and to fix its public finances, which are the worst among G7 economies.\par \par \uc2 BOJ REMAINS ON ALERT\par \par \uc2 Moody's\uc2  announcement came hours after G7 finance leaders signalled their readiness to take coordinated action against excessive and disorderly \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves.\par \par \uc2 A G7 telephone conference was arranged after worries of another U.S. recession and concern about the euro zone debt crisis hit global stocks and pushed the yen up near record highs, as investors sought the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a safe haven.\par \par \uc2 The yen's spike prompted Japan to sell 4.6 trillion yen ($59 billion) in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market last week and the BOJ to ease monetary policy to alleviate the pain to the export-reliant economy.\par \par \uc2 But the moves have failed to push the dollar sustainably above 80 yen -- the rate on which many manufacturers have based their earnings forecasts for the current fiscal year. It stood around 77.80 yen on Monday.\par \par \uc2 "We will continue to carefully monitor market moves. We also confirmed the G7 stance on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Noda told reporters after Monday's emergency G7 phone meeting.\par \par \uc2 He did not comment on whether they discussed the possibility of joint \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the dollar, but said \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  asked its G7 counterparts for their statement to make a clear mention of "issues related to the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market".\par \par \uc2 Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  refrained from draining funds that entered the market through last week's yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , thereby amplifying the effect of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and monetary easing.\par \par \uc2 The BOJ is ready to pump huge amounts of liquidity into the banking system on any signs of stress in the money markets, a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  source told Reuters.\par \par \uc2 Despite \uc2 Moody's\uc2  warnings, Japan may not have much choice but to rely on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and monetary easing to support its fragile economy.\par \par \uc2 Having just acted last week, the BOJ hopes to hold off on additional easing for now to save its limited policy options for when the economy faces bigger troubles.\par \par \uc2 But BOJ officials do not rule out easing policy further in the near term and are keeping an eye on a U.S. rate-setting meeting on Tuesday, which may trigger sharp yen rises against the dollar if the Federal Reserve signals additional monetary stimulus.\par \par \uc2 Noda expressed hopes that the BOJ will continue to support the economy with its ultra-loose monetary policy.\par \par \uc2 He also said market trust in the dollar and U.S. Treasuries had not wavered in the wake of S&P's downgrade, indicating that Tokyo will maintain its huge holdings of U.S. government bonds.\par \par \uc2 Japan held $912 billion in U.S. Treasuries as of May 2011, second only to China's $1.16 trillion in holdings, according to the U.S. Treasury Department.\par \par \uc2 Moody's\uc2  changed its outlook on Japan's Aa2 rating to negative from stable in February on concern that economic and fiscal policies would fall short of the tax reform needed to achieve deficit reduction targets and curb a rise in debt. ($1 = 78.490 Japanese Yen)\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Rie Ishiguro, Tetsushi Kajimoto and Kaori Kaneko; Editing by Nathan Layne and Edmund Klamann)\par \par \uc2 JAPAN-ECONOMY/NODA  (UPDATE 4)  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110811e78700008\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
