\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: Japan Official: G7 Meeting Could Be Held Before Asian Markets Reopen\b0\par\par\uc2 506 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 August 2011\par 06:34\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 --Japan official says telephone meeting of G7 finance ministers could be before Asian markets open on Monday\par \par \uc2 --Official says main agenda would be U.S. and European economies\par \par \uc2 --Another official says \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  against disruptive moves in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market\par \par \uc2 (Recasts, brings together comments by various Japanese officials.)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Kosaku Narioka and George Nishiyama \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Group of Seven rich nations are making preparations to hold a telephone meeting of their finance ministers as early as Monday, while \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  stands ready to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  again in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets, Japanese officials said Sunday, as financial markets brace for further turmoil following the U.S. debt downgrade over the weekend.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "I acknowledge that that's one way of thinking," a Japanese official said when asked about the possibility of a G7 meeting being held before Asian markets open Monday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Earlier on Sunday, senior G7 officials held talks over the telephone to prepare for the discussion among their finance ministers and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  governors, other Japanese officials said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Concerns over the outlook of the U.S. economy and the European sovereign-debt crisis have led to sharp falls in stock markets around the world, and have also pushed up the yen to near a record-high against the dollar as investors shift their money away from the dollar and the euro. The Japanese official said U.S. and European economies would be the main agenda of the G7 talks.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But he added that if asked, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would explain to G7 counterparts the yen-selling \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  it carried out last week to cap the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's rise.\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market on Thursday in tandem with an additional credit easing by the Bank of Japan, selling an estimated Y4.46 trillion to Y4.66 trillion--a record-high for a single day--to stem the yen's rise. But lingering concerns over the U.S. economy has kept upward pressure on the Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , and the downgrade of U.S. debt rating by \f28 \uc2 \f2 Standard & Poor's\f28 \uc2 \f2  over the weekend is expected to push it higher.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Senior Vice Finance Minister Fumihiko Igarashi said Tokyo was ready to step in again should there be disruptive moves in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  isn't over yet," Igarashi said in a televised debate program, adding: "If there are 'antimarket' speculative moves, we will take action again."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 He also said he was concerned that worries over the U.S. fiscal condition may lead to a global financial crisis, adding that he will closely watch how Washington would deal with its debt.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 While markets have focused on the debt problems faced by the U.S. and European nations, Japan is far from free from the issue, with its outstanding debt now nearly a twice of its economy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By George Nishiyama and Kosaku Narioka, Dow Jones Newswires; +81-3-6269-2818; george.nishiyama@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 --Takashi Nakamichi contributed to this article. [ 08-07-11 0034ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020110807e78700009\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
