\par\par \b \uc2 CORRECT: Asia Govts React Cautiously After S&P Downgrades U.S.\b0\par\par\uc2 806 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 August 2011\par 07:29\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 ("UPDATE: Asia Govts React Cautiously After S&P Downgrades U.S.," at 0409 GMT, misstated the U.S.'s previous rating in the first paragraph. The correct version follows:)\par \par \uc2 -- Asia governments react cautiously after S&P strips U.S. of its prized triple-A rating\par \par \uc2 -- PBOC adviser says the move will undercut the U.S. dollar\par \par \uc2 -- Japan says the attractiveness of U.S. Treasurys as an investment has not changed\par \par \uc2 -- Korea is watching the market reaction\par \par \uc2 By Takashi Nakamichi, Se Young Lee and Arran Scott\par \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES\par \par \uc2 Asian governments Saturday reacted cautiously to \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  decision to strip the U.S. of its prized triple-A rating, a move that could shake confidence in global financial markets and undercut the value of their massive holdings of U.S. Treasury bonds.\par \par \uc2 An adviser to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of China, the largest credit to the U.S. government, said the downgrade will damage the U.S. dollar.\par \par \uc2 S&P Friday cut the U.S. government debt rating from AAA to AA+ with a negative outlook, the first time in modern history that one of the three main ratings firms has stripped the U.S. of its AAA status. The move puts rating of the world's biggest economy below Liechtenstein and more than a dozen other countries, and on par with Belgium and New Zealand.\par \par \uc2 For Asian governments, the downgrade is bad news. Most countries in the region are running large trade surpluses with the U.S., resulting in a large accumulation of dollars, which they have ploughed into U.S. government and agency bonds. Any disruptions to the U.S. economy, a major market for Asian exporters, would also have serious implications for Asia's economy.\par \par \uc2 Japan, the second largest creditor to the U.S. after China, stood by its long-time ally, saying the attractiveness of U.S. Treasurys as an investment has not changed, despite the S&P move late Friday in New York.\par \par \uc2 "The trust we have in U.S. Treasurys and their attractiveness as an investment will not change because of this action," a senior Japanese government official told Dow Jones Newswires. Another official also said Japan's investment policy regarding its foreign reserves remains unchanged.\par \par \uc2 Xia Bin, an academic adviser to the People's Bank of China, said the depreciation trend for the U.S. dollar will continue. "The U.S. dollar will be in the depreciation trend in the long term," he said.\par \par \uc2 Yoon Jong-won, a senior official at South Korea's Ministry of Strategy and Finance, told Dow Jones that while the credit downgrade isn't a good news, local authorities will first have to see how the market reacts to the downgrade.\par \par \uc2 "It's not clear how much additional shock this downgrade will bring," he said. "We'll first have to see how the U.S. market reacts to the news in terms of how big a movement in capital occurs there and at what pace."\par \par \uc2 South Korean \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and government officials are scheduled to meet at 0700 GMT Sunday to discuss market conditions and how the government needs to respond.\par \par \uc2 Korean officials have acknowledged that the heightened concerns about U.S. and European economic health will affect the local economy and markets in the short term but have said that Korea's economic fundamentals remain sound.\par \par \uc2 Douglas C. Borthwick, managing director at Faros Trading, LLC said all eyes will be on China, saying any comments from Chinese Premier Wen Jiabao would likely to provide direction for financial markets when they open next week.\par \par \uc2 China has nearly $3.2 trillion of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, and some estimate that as much as 70% are in dollar denominated assets.\par \par \uc2 "We expect this to be dollar negative across the board," said Borthwick.\par \par \uc2 Analysts say Japan is unlikely to sell any of its dollar-denominated assets at a time when it is fighting to stem the yen's rise to near a record-high against the greenback.\par \par \uc2 On Thursday, together with further credit easing by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the finance ministry \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, selling an estimated Y4.5 trillion, double the previous one-day record for a yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The yen sales, most likely against the dollar, will further increase its foreign reserves, which hit a record high of Y1.151 trillion at the end of July. Of the total, Y1.060 trillion yen was held in securities, most of which are believed to be U.S. Treasurys, finance ministry data showed.\par \par \uc2 -By Takashi Nakamichi and Se Young Lee, Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 -- Natasha Brereton in Singapore, Eliot Gao in Beijing and Javier E. David in New York contributed to this article [ 08-06-11 0129ET ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020110806e78600012\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
