\par\par \b \uc2 Net Euro Long Position $314 Million, Decreases 90%--CFTC\b0\par\par\uc2 By Chana R. Schoenberger   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 588 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 August 2011\par 21:29\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--When \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  speculators look at the world's markets, they see few scenarios that could be negative for the yen. In the week ended Tuesday, Aug. 2, investors poured $9.5 billion into bets that the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will appreciate, creating the largest net-long yen position against the dollar since at least January 2007, 16% above the previous high.\par \par \uc2 The data, from the Commodity Futures Trading Commission's weekly report on the commitments of traders, reveal starkly the forces arrayed against Japanese exporters in the days leading up to \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Wednesday moves to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , in which the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold its own \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken it against the dollar. The country's government has been increasingly concerned that the strength of the yen is hobbling its manufacturing sector as \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders, spooked by the debt crises roiling the euro zone, seek refuge in \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "Clearly they were unfazed by the potential for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Camilla Sutton, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Scotia Capital in Toronto. The Japanese government, she said, likely considered this data as one factor when they elected to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  this week: "Speculation in markets have taken valuations beyond where they would rationally be, and they hope to shake out some of those speculative longs from the market."\par \par \uc2 Tuesday also marked the end, if only temporarily, to the U.S. debt-ceiling debate, which climaxed with a deal to increase the government's borrowing capabilities signed at the eleventh hour. The acrimonious political battle over the debt limit colored the market's continued net short against the dollar, which stood at $27 billion, down just 1% from the previous week. Traders hold every other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  long against the dollar, which has been on a long-term downward slide.\par \par \uc2 The euro, which has suffered from a massive loss in investor confidence as member nations fight off sovereign-debt troubles, saw its net long position wither by 90%, to $314 million, or 1,763 contracts.\par \par \uc2 Much of the money fleeing the euro zone moved next door to Switzerland, which investors view as a refuge from the euro's troubles. The Swiss government was also forced to defend its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and export sector on Tuesday, announcing a quantitative easing program that Geneva hopes will weaken the Swiss franc, which has also hit record highs recently. CFTC data showed the market held a net long position of $2 billion in the Swiss franc, up 61%, at 12,341 contracts.\par \par \uc2 Investors were three-times more bullish on the British pound this week, but continued to hold a relatively small net long position of $532 million in sterling. The U.K. has benefited from its position outside the euro zone, but it faces its own economic challenges, making investors somewhat wary of the pound.\par \par \uc2 Commodity-linked \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  held fairly steady, with net long positions in the Canadian and New Zealand dollars up 12% and 5% respectively and the Australian dollar down 6%. While concerns about global growth have hit these \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , speculators also see the resource-based economies as less vulnerable to the debt crises of the developed world.\par \par \uc2 The CFTC's weekly data report shows speculative investors' positions in major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  held against the dollar. It viewed the markets through Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 -By Chana R. Schoenberger, Dow Jones Newswires; 212-416-4803; chana.schoenberger@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 -Stephen L. Bernard contributed to this article. [ 05-08-11 2030GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110805e785001rj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
