\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE:  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  Finance Minister:  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Not Aimed At Certain  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Level\b0\par\par\uc2 328 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 August 2011\par 03:38\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    -- \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's finance minister says \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  not aimed at certain level \par    -- Minister says need to watch stock market as well \par    -- Minister won't comment on remarks by overseas officials on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 (Adds finance minister's comments throughout)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By George Nishiyama \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Japan's finance minister said Friday that the Japanese authorities weren't aiming for a certain level of exchange rates in their market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to stem the yen's strength.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda also said he will closely watch developments in the stock market following the overnight fall in the Dow Jones Industrial Average.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as a measure to suppress speculative and disorderly moves," Noda told a news conference. "It is not an \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  aimed at a certain level," he added.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We will continue to watch the market and take timely and appropriate measures."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Noda also said he would pay particular attention to the stock market Friday following an overnight fall in U.S. markets.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As of 0130 GMT, the benchmark Nikkei average was down 3.5% at 9320.55, while the dollar was at Y78.90 after rising above Y80 overnight.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Noda declined to comment on remarks by foreign officials that they didn't support \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's unilateral \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market. But he added he had been in touch with his overseas counterparts.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Following Japan's action Thursday, European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  President Jean-Claude Trichet said \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  must be conducted on a multilateral basis, while a U.S. official told Dow Jones Newswires that Washington didn't back Japan's decision.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Asked whether the G-20 needed to hold talks on the global economy, given the overnight stock selloff, Noda said they should do so when the group meets in September, but didn't elaborate.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By George Nishiyama, Dow Jones Newswires; +81-3-6269-2818; george.nishiyama@dowjones.com [ 05-08-11 0138GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110805e7850004p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
