\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  OUTLOOK-US dollar could weaken as Fed meeting looms\b0\par\par\uc2 727 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 August 2011\par 23:09\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Fed meeting, assessment of economy will be key focus\par \par \uc2 * Swiss franc, yen stay in favor on economic worries\par \par \uc2 * Investors on alert for more \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 (Recasts; add comment, updates prices)\par \par \uc2 By Wanfeng Zhou\par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Aug 5 (Reuters) - The U.S. dollar could fall next week, especially against the Swiss franc and Japanese yen, as investors look to a Federal Reserve meeting for hints of further easing as worries about the global economy grow.\par \par \uc2 Stocks sold off and safe-haven assets, like the Swiss franc, soared this week after weak data fueled fears the world economy is slipping back into a recession. That sentiment persisted even after data Friday showed U.S. job growth accelerated more than expected last month.\par \par \uc2 The Swiss franc climbed to record highs against the dollar and euro on Friday, before easing back. The dollar last traded up 0.2 percent at 0.7665 franc <CHF=EBS>, while the euro rose 1.5 percent at 1.0960 francs <EURCHF=EBS>.\par \par \uc2 For the week, the dollar lost about 3.0 percent against the Swiss \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the euro dropped 3.7 percent.\par \par \uc2 Against the yen <JPY=>, the dollar was last at 78.49, down 0.7 percent on the day, but rose 1.2 percent on the week after \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  and eased monetary policy to weaken the yen.\par \par \uc2 Sharp moves in financial markets and the spike in volatility prompted aggressive policy actions by several major central banks in recent days, including a surprise interest rate cut by Switzerland, yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and a resumption of bond buying by the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "With markets already on the defensive and the macroeconomic picture deteriorating, (Fed Chairman Ben) Bernanke cannot afford to be tight-lipped about further quantitative easing," said Ashraf Laidi, CEO of Intermarket Strategy Ltd. in London.\par \par \uc2 "They have to continue signaling their readiness to introduce further measures, regardless of the Fed's opinion about the cost-benefit analysis of further quantitative easing," he added.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. Federal Open Market Committee, the Fed's policy-setting panel, is slated to announce its decision on interest rates at 2:15 p.m. EDT (1815 GMT) on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 With no change in rates expected, investors will scrutinize the accompanying statement for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's assessment on the economy and the outlook for monetary policy.\par \par \uc2 Any hints that the Fed could introduce news measures to stimulate a slowing economy could further erode the dollar's traditional role as a safe haven, driving investors to the Swiss franc and Japanese yen.\par \par \uc2 "Even if the Fed doesn't talk about QE3 next week, the market is going to continue to leave it as a possibility," said David Watt, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 RBC Capital Markets\uc2  in Toronto. "The only thing can turn the U.S. dollar trend is we start seeing a string of upward surprises in U.S. economic data."\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  WATCH\par \par \uc2 Japanese Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda said he was closely watching yen moves on Friday, signaling a readiness to continue selling the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . For details, see [ID:nL3E7J467X]\par \par \uc2 "There is a good likelihood they will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again as they will want to make it clear to the markets that they will not be tested and they were not one-off \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Andrew Cox, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  in New York.\par \par \uc2 But with worries about a global economic slowdown, analysts doubted actions by central banks will spark a trend reversal.\par \par \uc2 The euro last traded up 1.3 at $1.4290 <EUR=EBS> but was down slightly on the week. It got a boost after Italy's Prime Minister Silvio Berlusconi pledged on Friday to speed up austerity measures and bring the country's budget into balance by 2013. [ID:nR1E7IF02F]\par \par \uc2 Sources told Reuters the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had demanded such measures in exchange for buying bonds to ease the pressure on Italy, which has come under market attack.\par \par \uc2 But Andrew Busch, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at BMO Capital Markets, said any positive sentiment would fade.\par \par \uc2 "Seriously, can the markets or the ECB trust Italian PM Berlusconi to put through reforms?" he said. "Remember, the Europeans have yet to implement their last rescue scheme for Greece and the ratings agencies are still likely to downgrade Italy and Spain."\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 /OUTLOOK   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110805e7850024k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
