\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 679 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 August 2011\par 04:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par UPDATE: \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 JAPAN\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  YOSANO: PREMATURE TO ASSUME \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCY INTERVENTION\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  IS ONE-TIME DEAL \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's economy minister said Friday it would be premature to assume yesterday's yen-selling \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was a one-time measure, suggesting the Finance Ministry will step into the market again to stem the Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's rise if needed. (published 0132 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 JAPAN\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  FINANCE MINISTER: \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENTION\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  NOT AIMED AT CERTAIN \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  LEVEL \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's finance minister said Friday that the Japanese authorities weren't aiming for a certain level of exchange rates in their market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to stem the yen's strength. (published 0138 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: NO BOJ DRAINING SUGGESTS \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX INTERVENTION\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  FUNDS BEING LEFT IN MARKET \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , as largely expected by market players, refrained from draining funds from the market at its Friday morning money market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , suggesting that it is not at this point sterilizing the yen-selling \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the government on the previous day. (published 0139 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par RBA CUTS 2011 GROWTH OUTLOOK BUT REBOUND LIKELY SWIFT; INFLATION TROUBLING \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australia's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Friday revised sharply lower its forecast for economic growth in 2011, but said it continues to expect a full-blooded recovery in 2012, while inflation is set to remain a nagging problem for some years. (published 0130 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par FITCH'S COLQUHOUN: JAPAN'S POLICY FLEXIBILITY IS A SUPPORT 'AT THE MARGIN' \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's policy flexibility, as demonstrated by its latest easing measures and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , is a support for the sovereign "at the margin," a senior analyst at Fitch Ratings told to Dow Jones Newswires Friday. (published 0124 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CHINA RESEARCHER: CHINA'S 2011 INFLATION MAY TOP 5% - REPORT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's inflation rate may climb above 5% this year, although the pace of price rises may ease slightly in the second half of the year, a state researcher said in a newspaper commentary Friday. (published 0153 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CHINA RESEARCHER: SHOULD STOP RAISING BANKS' RESERVE REQUIREMENT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China should stop raising the reserve requirement for banks, and consider lowering the requirement for small- and medium-sized banks when inflation falls back to below 4%, a researcher with a government think-tank wrote in an commentary in the Economic Information Daily Friday. (published 0152 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par IMF: SOUTH KOREAN ECONOMIC OUTLOOK REMAINS 'FAVORABLE' \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korea's economic outlook remains "favorable," with growth expected to remain robust, allowing policy makers to begin tightening monetary policy, the International Monetary Fund said Thursday in a report. (published 0003 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par AUSTRALIA'S SWAN SAYS ECONOMIC FUNDAMENTALS REMAIN STRONG \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australia's Deputy Prime Minister and Treasurer Wayne Swan said Friday while the country isn't immune to the dislocation sweeping through global markets, the underlying economy remains strong with growth in the Asia region. (published 2338 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par AUSTRALIA JULY PERFORMANCE OF CONSTRUCTION INDEX +0.3 PTS TO 36.1 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australia's construction sector continued to contract in July for the 14th consecutive month, dragged by weak home building, a performance gauge produced by an industry group published Friday shows. (published 2330 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CHINA SHOULDN'T CHANGE MONETARY POLICY DESPITE COMPLAINTS - PAPER \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China shouldn't make a big change to its monetary policy despite "some voices" calling for a loosening, a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 -backed paper argued in an editorial Friday. (published 0043 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CHINA RESEARCHER: CHINA ECONOMY NEEDS TO SLOW SLIGHTLY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's economy needs to slow slightly and stabilize, a researcher with a government think-tank wrote in a commentary in the China Securities Journal Friday. (published 0019 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par DATA SNAP: PHILIPPINE JULY INFLATION 4.6% ON YEAR \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Inflation in the Philippines stood at 4.6% in July, supported by food, fuel and clothing prices as well as utility rates, data released by the National Statistics Office Friday showed. (published 0121 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par     ======= DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ======= \par  \par  \par WSJ: CAPITALS ON GUARD AGAINST RISING \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 No country seems to want a rising \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 --and that poses a problem for a world economy struggling with weakening prospects for growth in the world's biggest developed nations. (published 2344 GMT) [ 05-08-11 0200GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110805e7850005u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
