\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ: Japan Moves To Weaken The Yen\b0\par\par\uc2 By Takashi Nakamichi and Chester Dawson  \par \uc2 Of THE WALL STREET JOURNAL  \par \uc2 1,206 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 August 2011\par 03:43\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--The Japanese government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  Thursday morning in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to stem the rise of the yen against the dollar, hoping to preserve modest growth in an economy rebounding surprisingly well from the March earthquake and tsunami--but that now appears threatened by a soaring \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  undermining exporters.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 --the third move by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  in less than a year to soften the yen's steady strengthening--follows by a day a move by the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  to push down its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , showing a spreading global response to the renewed weakness in the U.S., and rising doubts by investors worldwide about the soundness of American economic policy. Leaders around the world in recent days have decried the messy debate in Washington to avoid a debt default, while credit-rating companies have threatened to downgrade the U.S. sovereign debt. Some have called for a search for new reserve \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to reduce the global role of the weakening dollar.\par \par \uc2 (This story and related background material will be available on The Wall Street Journal website, WSJ.com.)\par \par \uc2 Despite Japan's own woes and heavy government borrowing, the yen has emerged as a haven for investors facing growing uncertainty in the U.S. and Europe. Before the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the dollar had dropped below 77 yen, close to the postwar record of Y76.25 touched the week after the March earthquake. Immediately after Tokyo's move, the rate rose a full yen and the dollar was worth about Y78. In June 2010, the dollar was worth more than Y90, about 17% stronger than today.\par \par \uc2 Beyond stepping into \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, Japanese policy makers are mulling other options to keep their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in check. The Bank of Japan is holding a regular meeting Thursday, and officials announced they would cut short their session, scheduled originally to run through Friday. Officials are reportedly discussion trying to weaken the yen by further easing credit through increasing the size of a fund it uses to buy assets like government and corporate bonds. Action remains unclear.\par \par \uc2 Thursday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Finance Ministry underscores a renewed \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  activism by Japan as the yen has danced near record highs over the past year.\par \par \uc2 Before last year, Japan had gone more than six years without buying or selling \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to alter the value. On Sept. 15, 2010, with the dollar worth about Y83, the Japanese government executed its largest-ever single-day yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . In conjunction with BOJ easing, that helped stabilize the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for a time.\par \par \uc2 A sharp spike in the value of the yen after the March 11 natural disasters prompted joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Group of Seven on March 18. The dollar strengthened in the following weeks, before new U.S. weakness pushed the greenback down again in recent days.\par \par \uc2 Despite the dramatic move, it is unclear how successful \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be, especially since Thursday's move appears to have been carried out without backup from the other G-7 countries. While Japanese officials suggested in recent days they would have the tacit support of the U.S. and other countries for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the action could also trigger complaints of a new round of competitive devaluations, particularly from Asian trading rivals such as South Korea.\par \par \uc2 Underlying economic conditions could also limit the impact of any Japanese move. Despite assertions from Japanese officials that exchange rates don't reflect fundamentals, the dollar's weakness has been driven by expectations of ongoing easy-money policy from the Federal Reserve, which has been more aggressive than the BOJ in recent months in flooding the economy with liquidity, a comparative stance that tends to cheapen the dollar versus other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Japan's persistently large trade surplus has also worked to keep the yen strong.\par \par \uc2 Still, Japanese officials were driven to act as Japanese companies have reacted with increasing alarm to the yen's steady rise against the dollar and other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . While lowering the cost of some critical imports, including oil and food items, the elevated Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has eroded profits at Japan's many exporters. A strong yen hurts Japan Inc.'s competitiveness by lowering the yen-value of revenue earned in dollars and making products more expensive overseas.\par \par \uc2 A litany of executives at brand-name companies such as \uc2 Nissan Motor Co\uc2 . (NSANY, 7201.TO), \uc2 Panasonic Corp\uc2 . (PC, 6752.TO), and \uc2 Toshiba Corp\uc2 . (TOSYY, 6502.TO) warned last week during first-quarter earnings press conferences that it has become increasingly difficult to maintain their current production levels in Japan amid the high yen.\par \par \uc2 "The current strong yen is out of reach of an individual company to deal with," Nissan corporate vice president \uc2 Joji Tagawa\uc2  said last week.\par \par \uc2 One notable exception is \uc2 Sony Corp\uc2 . (SNE, 6758.TO), which suffered no losses from the yen's strength after off-shoring almost all of its dollar-denominated sales. That decision, albeit one taken over a 20-year period, may be a harbinger of more hollowing out to come for Japan's vaunted industrial base.\par \par \uc2 Indeed, Corporate Japan's balance sheets indicate few companies expect the yen to weaken rapidly. Many blue chip Japanese exporters, including \uc2 Honda Motor Co\uc2 . (HMC, 7267.TO), \uc2 Canon Inc\uc2 . (CAJ, 7751.TO) and \uc2 Nintendo Co\uc2 . (NTDOY, 7974.OK) based their full year earnings forecasts at an assumed exchange rate of Y80 to the dollar. That represents a painful multi-year high, but also signals they don't expect the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to move any closer to the Y70 level in the balance of the fiscal year through next March.\par \par \uc2 And, despite the economic drag, a strong yen has also had some benefits for corporate Japan. It has lowered the relative price of foreign companies for Japanese firms looking to make overseas acquisitions.\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, in a surprise effort to fend off a flood of money entering the country from investors seeking a safe haven, the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  cut interest rates to nearly zero and pledged to pump billions of newly minted francs into its markets.\par \par \uc2 Calling its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  "massively overvalued," the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it "will take further measures against the strength of the Swiss franc if necessary."\par \par \uc2 Investors seeking refuge from financial-market turmoil have for months been turning to countries such as Switzerland and Japan, driving up their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and threatening to weigh on their economies.\par \par \uc2 Switzerland's move, which analysts doubted would have lasting success as long as uncertainty dominates global markets, reflects another in a growing list of knock-on effects from the 2008 financial crisis and its aftermath.\par \par \uc2 Prior to Wednesday's move, the franc had risen 11% against the dollar in just the past month and 13% against the euro. On Wednesday the Swiss franc fell 0.6% against the dollar and 1.6% against the euro.\par \par \uc2 --Tom Lauricella and Neil Shah contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Order free Annual Report for \uc2 Honda Motor Company Ltd\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Visit {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://djnewswires.ar.wilink.com/?link=HMC"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://djnewswires.ar.wilink.com/?link=HMC }}}\uc2  or call 1-888-301-0513\par \par \uc2 Order free Annual Report for \uc2 Sony Corp\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Visit {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://djnewswires.ar.wilink.com/?link=SNE"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://djnewswires.ar.wilink.com/?link=SNE }}}\uc2  or call 1-888-301-0513 [ 08-03-11 2153ET ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020110804e7840000h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
