\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: US Didn't Support  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2 's  \highlight22\uc2 Currency Intervention \highlight\uc2  -Official\b0\par\par\uc2 675 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 August 2011\par 15:08\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 --Lack of U.S. backing suggests joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  not imminent\par \par \uc2 --G-7 deputies in contact, but no suggestion of coordinated action\par \par \uc2 --U.S. stance hasn't changed since before the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 (Updates with comments from European official in paragraphs five to seven, adds background starting in paragraph eight)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Tom Barkley and Alistair MacDonald \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 WASHINGTON (Dow Jones)--The U.S. didn't back \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's decision to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  overnight in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets, an official told Dow Jones Newswires Thursday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.S official said the Obama administration didn't support the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , which sought to weaken the yen to help support \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's economy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The official confirmed that the U.S stance hasn't changed from earlier in the week, when a person familiar with the matter made it clear that potential action from Japan wouldn't win the Obama administration's endorsement. That suggests that a more coordinated \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  involving the U.S. to boost the dollar isn't imminent, though Japan has made clear it is willing to go it alone.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Strong flows into safe-harbor \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  like the yen and Swiss franc, caused in large part by concerns over the debt burdens of European countries and the U.S., have prompted market responses from those governments and fueled speculation of some type of global coordinated action.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Deputy officials from Group of Seven leading nations spoke by phone again Wednesday, according to a European official familiar with the discussions.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But while the major economies have remained in constant contact as the sovereign-debt crisis has escalated, there hasn't been any suggestion of near-term moves by the G7, the official said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The official said it's difficult to gauge the significance of the market volatility given thin trading conditions. Since euro-zone sovereign debt troubles are viewed as the main driver behind the flight to quality, it's still mostly seen as Europe's problem to handle, the official said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda said after the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was launched that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was acting by itself, warning that he would continue to "closely watch" the market. He planned to keep in touch with officials overseas, but said he would do what he thinks is necessary to help the economy recover from the earthquake and tsunami in March.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.S. largely looked the other way when \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  last September, though when asked about it the following month, a senior Treasury official said the U.S. stance remained that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  should be based on market fundamentals.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 However, the March 11 earthquake and tsunami in Japan caused a major shift in market forces and world sympathy toward Japan, with the G-7 vowing to cooperate further on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy as needed following multilateral \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on March 18.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On Wednesday, before setting out on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , Noda held up the 1995 campaign to cap yen strength as a "useful reference" for the Japan's current stance. That effort, largely viewed as successful, was marked by large, less frequent bouts of yen sales in which Japan mostly acted alone but at times organized a coordinated response.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bank of Japan so far Thursday is estimated to have spent between $20 billion and $30 billion on behalf of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to curb yen appreciation, which would top the amounts spent in March and September.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar was pushed up above Y80 against the yen for the first time since July 12, from Y77.13 before the Bank of Japan entered the market.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The yen sales have continued during the day, though the dollar has settled back below Y80, considered a pain threshold for Japanese exporters. At 8:20 a.m. EDT, the dollar was at Y79.56.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Tom Barkley and Alistair MacDonald Dow Jones Newswires; 202-862-9275, tom.barkley@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 --Takashi Mochizuki, Takashi Nakamichi and Megumi Fujikawa in Tokyo, Jessica Mead in London, and Ian Talley in Washington contributed to this article. [ 08-04-11 0908ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020110804e784000dx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
