\par\par \b \uc2 DJ UPDATE: Japan Finance Minister Hints At More Efforts To Weaken Yen\b0\par\par\uc2 401 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 August 2011\par 11:23\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 -- Japan's finance minister says government is "halfway" on efforts to weaken yen\par \par \uc2 -- Says will do what is deemed necessary regarding yen\par \par \uc2 -- Vows to keep close watch on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market\par \par \uc2 -- Says will keep in touch with overseas \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities\par \par \uc2 (Adds finance minister's comments throughout) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Takashi Nakamichi \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's finance minister hinted Thursday that the government will \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  further in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market if necessary, saying its efforts to weaken the yen are "still halfway" and that he will "do what is deemed necessary."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Earlier in the day, the Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sold the yen for the first time since March, while the Bank of Japan announced additional monetary easing, coordinating efforts to stem export-damaging rises in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Asked about the impact of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  so far, Yoshihiko Noda said: "We are still halfway through, so I'll sum up the result after this is over."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Although Noda emphasized that the government isn't aiming to guide the yen to any specific levels, his comments suggest that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's latest round of unilateral yen-selling \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 --started in early Asian trading hours--could continue until the Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  weakens to a certain extent. Traders say the government is still selling the yen during London hours.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Noda also vowed to "continue to closely watch the market" and "firmly do what is deemed necessary."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 He kept tightlipped about the possibility of other nations joining the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  efforts, but he said that "given current international financial conditions, I'd like to always stay in contact" with overseas authorities.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Noda said the ministry and the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  "acted in concert," and he hopes that the additional monetary easing by the BOJ will help weaken the yen.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "I believe (the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ) acted in an appropriate and timely manner, by taking into consideration those financial and economic changes such as the yen's accelerating upward trend," Noda said. "I hope those steps will have a clear impact. The government values them."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Noda said he has been in touch with BOJ Gov. Masaaki Shirakawa, but declined to comment on when they talked with each other.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Takashi Nakamichi, Dow Jones Newswires; +81-3-6269-2818; takashi.nakamichi@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 August 04, 2011 05:23 ET (09:23 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020110804e784002l7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
