\par\par \b \uc2 4th UPDATE:  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  Launches  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  to Push Down Yen; BOJ Eases Further\b0\par\par\uc2 886 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 August 2011\par 14:24\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 -- \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to push down yen\par \par \uc2 -- Finance minister says action was needed to stop "one-sided" moves in market\par \par \uc2 -- Dealers estimate amount at nearly Y1 trillion\par \par \uc2 -- Dollar rises more than Y2.5 to around Y79.80\par \par \uc2 -- BOJ announces Y10 trillion in new easing after shortened policy board meeting\par \par \uc2 (Adds later Noda comments, reaction from corporate leaders, 5th-6th, 17th-22nd paragraphs)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Takashi Mochizuki, Takashi Nakamichi and Megumi Fujikawa \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Japan stepped into the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market Thursday, launching a yen-selling campaign that was backed up by Y10 trillion in easing measures from the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as officials say they needed to tame market speculation threatening a fragile economic recovery.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But even as the authorities plowed money into the markets, keeping the pressure up through the day and into European hours, traders and analysts said it was far from certain that the authorities would succeed in heading off the yen's steady climb and halting the damage it is doing to the economy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The government plunged into the market at 0100 GMT via the Bank of Japan, causing the yen to spike lower against other key \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . The dollar quickly jumping to Y78.20 from Y77.13 and the euro rose to Y111.80 from Y110.72.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 To underscore its conviction, the government continued selling yen through the day, pushing the dollar to Y79.48. At 1220 GMT the dollar was at Y79.56.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda, who had earlier announced the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at a hastily called news conference in the morning, hinted Thursday that the government won't let up.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Asked about the impact of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  so far, Noda said: "We are still halfway through, so I'll sum up the result after this is over."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Some market watchers also predicted a long campaign. "What we've seen is a skirmish in what could become a war of attrition," said Robert Rennie, chief \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Westpac Banking Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2 . in Sydney.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Despite recent talk of global cooperation, Noda said Japan acted alone. A U.S. official confirmed later that Washington hadn't taken part.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As trading shifted to Europe, dealers in Tokyo estimated that Japan had spent just under Y1 trillion and fresh orders were increasing in size as the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  continued.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "They are very serious," said one Tokyo trader.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bolstering the government measures, the Bank of Japan said it would pour another Y10 trillion into the economy by increasing its asset purchase fund by Y5 trillion and adding another Y5 trillion in fixed-rate six-month loans, which currently total Y10 trillion.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The BOJ will likely remain ready to ease further," said Naomi Hasegawa, a senior fixed income strategist at Mitsubishi UFJ Morgan Stanley Securities.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 While not directly aimed at affecting \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rates, adding more money to an economy tends to push down the value of its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The yen's recent strength has clouded the outlook for Japan's export-driven economy as it recovers from the March 11 earthquake and tsunami, drawing increasing complaints from big Japanese exporters.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Despite Japan's relatively bleak economic prospects and mammoth government borrowing, the yen has emerged as a safe haven for investors facing growing uncertainty in the U.S. and Europe.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 With interest rates near zero and an emphasis on having a stable monetary policy, the Bank of Japan is considered a "safe pair of hands" that won't taken any drastic steps that could undermine market confidence.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The move brought some support from business leaders who have been complaining about the strong yen.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Kazuo Hirai\f28 \uc2 \f2 , who oversees \f28 \uc2 \f2 Sony Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2 .'s (6758.TO) electronics and videogame business, cheered the move to slow the yen's sudden rise.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The fact that the government \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in a market where the changes were so rapid is a good thing for Japanese industry as a whole," he said during a meeting with reporters.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Sony\f28 \uc2 \f2 , like other companies, has sought to offset the high value of the yen but moving more of its cost base to countries where it receives large amounts of revenue, such as the U.S. However, the appreciating yen hurts \f28 \uc2 \f2 Sony\f28 \uc2 \f2 's competitiveness with Korean rivals such as \f28 \uc2 \f2 Samsung Electronics Co\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (005930.SE) and \f28 \uc2 \f2 LG Electronics\f28 \uc2 \f2  (066570.SE).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Other business leaders were somewhat more grudging, describing the move as too late as the yen had risen to near-record highs.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  seems slightly late, but (the government) has done well," said Hiromasa Yonekura, chairman of Nippon Keidanren, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's biggest business lobby.\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  last \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  just over four months ago after the dollar dropped to a post-World War II low of Y76.25 on March 17. The March 11 disaster put upward pressure on the yen by leading to speculation Japanese firms operating overseas would repatriate funds to meet reconstruction and other costs.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 At that time, Japan's finance ministry sold Y692.5 billion, following a rare declaration of joint \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the Group of Seven economic powers.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Takashi Mochizuki, Takashi Nakamichi, Andrew Monahan; Dow Jones Newswires; +81-3-6269-2782; takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Tatsuo Ito in Tokyo and Leslie Shaffer and Martin Vaughan in Singapore contributed to this story [ 04-08-11 1224GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110804e7840017l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
